\par\par \b \uc2 DJ China Yuan Ends Lower As Speculation For Rise Subsides  \b0\par\par\uc2 403 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juni 2006\par 11:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2006, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan closed lower against the U.S. dollar Friday, remaining range-bound as speculation the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would allow the yuan to resume its appreciation subsided.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the U.S. dollar closed at CNY8.0215 on the automatic price-matching system after trading in a range of CNY8.0239 and CNY8.0200. On Thursday it closed at CNY8.0200.  \par \par \uc2 On the over-the-counter market the dollar was at CNY8.0230 at 0730 GMT. It traded between CNY8.0230 and CNY8.0195. On Thursday it closed at CNY8.0204.  \par \par \uc2 The pair didn't stray far from the central parity rate for the dollar-yuan, set at 8.0214 Friday morning.  \par \par \uc2 'The rate still seems stuck in its recent range. There's been nothing to get it moving,' said a Shanghai-based dealer with a local bank.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has mainly traded between CNY8.02 and CNY8.03 for the last two weeks. However, in recent sessions it pushed past the lower end of that range, closing at CNY8.0175 Wednesday following the release of a People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  report that said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  less in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as it matures.  \par \par \uc2 Some analysts took this as a signal the PBOC was set to allow the pace of the yuan's appreciation to pick up. But by Friday, this speculation had subsided.  \par \par \uc2 'The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is rarely so obvious,' said a Shanghai-based dealer with a foreign bank when asked if the report indicated the yuan was set to rise.  \par \par \uc2 Offshore, nondeliverable forwards were steady. Traders said it is likely the market's recent rise has ended.  \par \par \uc2 'The market's becoming more confident and we're seeing some buying interest coming back in,' said a Singapore-based trader.  \par \par \uc2 She said the end of the investor exodus from emerging markets had helped change the mood, adding traders will be closely watching next week's Group of Eight leading nations meeting to see if the G-8 increases its pressure on China to allow the yuan to rise faster.  \par \par \uc2 The offshore bid/offer quote for one-year dollar-yuan nondeliverable forwards was 7.7210/7.7240, steady from 7.7210/7.7260 late Thursday. -By Denis McMahon, Dow Jones Newswires; (86-21) 6120-1200; denis.mcmahon@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Theresa Davidovitz  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020060602e262004s9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
