\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  Less In Yuan Market Over Time  \b0\par\par\uc2 766 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Mai 2006\par 12:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Update with remarks from commerce minister and government economist on yuan, market moves)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Wednesday that it plans over time to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  less in markets to control its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exchange rate, but it also signaled that it isn't going to let the yuan appreciate at a sharply faster pace.  \par \par \uc2 The comments aren't a change in China's oft-stated policy on the yuan, but Beijing's choice of words is more direct than it has been in the past and were taken as a signal by \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation could pick up pace.  \par \par \uc2 In its monetary policy report for the first quarter, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it needs to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to smooth volatility in the early stages of the yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reform.  \par \par \uc2 "As reform of the exchange rate mechanism continues to deepen, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market supply and demand will trend toward balance and the behavior of market participants will also gradually mature," the bank said.  \par \par \uc2 "Complementing the market's growing strength, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's frequency and degree of (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) in the open market will continue to diminish, and gradually dwindle," it said.  \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the U.S. dollar closed at CNY8.0205 on the automatic price matching system, down from CNY8.0266 in the previous session.  \par \par \uc2 Including China's 2.1% revaluation of the yuan against the dollar last July, the dollar has fallen by only 3.1% versus the yuan since that time.  \par \par \uc2 Underpinning the yuan's strength is a robust economy that expanded at a 10.3% on-year clip in the first three months of 2006. Unabated gains in China's trade surplus and a growing pile of foreign reserves also support the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 Following the PBOC's report, the dollar fell by half a yen to a low of Y111.46, but then later recovered. At 0955 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y112.10.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Further CNY Revaluation Calls Groundless \par      \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Adding to market expectations of further yuan appreciation, an economist at the State Information Center told Dow Jones Newswires that China's economy can bear a 5% appreciation in the yuan against the dollar by late July this year from its pre-revaluation level  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Zhu Baoliang said that the domestic economy can also withstand a 10% appreciation in the yuan by July 2007 from its level of CNY8.28 against the dollar before it was revalued July 21 last year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Such a speed is appropriate and acceptable," Zhu said. "I came up with this conclusion after evaluating the possible impact on imports and exports, employment, price levels and balance of payments."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, China isn't likely to let the yuan rise quickly and, as it has said many times, will set its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy on domestic needs.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In an interview with Japanese media posted on China's Ministry of Commerce Web site, Commerce Minister Bo Xilai said China isn't manipulating the yuan exchange rate and there are no grounds to call for a further revaluation of the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Improving the yuan exchange rate mechanism is necessary to deepening China's financial reforms," said Bo.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To the international community, Bo reiterated China's view that Beijing can't resolve global trade imbalances alone. Appreciation of the yuan can help reduce the Sino-U.S. trade imbalance, but it would only have limited effect, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China critics, namely those in the U.S., have continued to insist China isn't moving fast enough on allowing the yuan more flexibility, which means Chinese goods are artificially cheap compared to foreign goods.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S., whose trade deficit with China has been widening, has pressed China to let the yuan float more freely and appreciate more quickly against the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. Treasury's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  report, issued earlier this month, didn't name China as a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulator, but said China had shown "too little progress" in making the yuan more flexible.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There isn't yet any indication of whether that U.S. pressure will change should U.S. President George W. Bush's nomination Tuesday of Henry Paulson to replace Treasury Secretary John Snow be successful.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Paulson, who is Goldman Sachs Chief Executive, has often said he isn't too worried about the U.S. trade deficit, which he expects to adjust from market forces over time.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By J.R. Wu, Dow Jones Newswires; 8610 6588-5848; jr.wu@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Iain McDonald [ 31-05-06 1025GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060531e25v000l3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
