\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Treasury Doesn't Name China As  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Manipulator  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,223 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Mai 2006\par 09:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2006, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, May 11, 2006 (DJCS via Comtex) --  \par \par \uc2 (This article was originally published Wednesday)  \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Elizabeth Price\par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. Treasury Department on Wednesday again declined to name China a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulator in its twice-yearly report to Congress on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, though Treasury said China had made "too little progress" in making its yuan more flexible.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The Treasury Department is unable to determine, from the evidence at hand, that China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  system was operated during the last half of 2005 for the purpose (i.e. with the intent) of preventing adjustments in China's balance of payments or gaining China an unfair competitive advantage in international trade," according to the report released Wednesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury pointed out a number of steps the Chinese government had taken to liberalize its financial markets, including China's decision last July to end its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  peg to the U.S. dollar and the slight appreciation of the yuan since then. China has also introduced measures, like interbank foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading and development of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  futures, to create the infrastructure needed to float the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The government has stated a commitment to strengthening domestic demand and easing its reliance on exports for growth. A stronger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would help China achieve those goals, Treasury said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "While these developments suggest that progress is being made, China's advances are far too slow and hesitant given China's own needs, and its responsibilities to the international financial community," Treasury said. "The delay in introducing additional exchange rate flexibility is unjustified given the strength of the Chinese economy and the progress in China's transition."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The hotly anticipated report, originally due in April, is a required account to Congress on the state of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in the second half of 2005. Treasury officials said they delayed the report to incorporate any new insights into Chinese policy gleaned from President George W. Bush's meeting with Chinese President Hu Jintao. Just ahead of the visit, China allowed the yuan to appreciate slightly, and it has crept along to stronger levels since then. On Wednesday in Asia, the yuan, which is also known as the renminbi, closed just slightly stronger against the dollar at CNY8.0033.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In its report, Treasury noted that China's trade surplus widened in the second half of 2005 and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves have grown to $875 billion as of March 31, 2006. It is a level "far in excess of amounts needed to cushion against adverse shocks," Treasury said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In its previous report last fall, Treasury had said China didn't meet the technical definition of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulation at that time, but would likely do so in the future if trends in reserves and trade continued and policy remained unchanged.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A designation of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulator would have triggered consultations between the U.S. and China, something the Bush administration says it is already doing in frequent meetings with Chinese officials at many levels of government. The most likely outcome of such a designation, say analysts, would be to embolden China's critics in Congress who want to put tariffs on Chinese imports in retaliation for its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sen. Charles Schumer, D-N.Y., who agreed to delay a vote on his legislation that would impose 27.5% tariffs on imports from China after a visit to Beijing in March, said his bill might be the best way to get China to move.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The administration has now made it a habit of walking up to the line and not crossing it, even in the face of overwhelming evidence," Schumer said. "The dodge that China has not met the 'technical' definition of manipulation will not satisfy Congress or the American people."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sen. Charles Grassley, R-Iowa, has a competing bill to pressure China over its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . He said Treasury's report underscored a need for an overhaul of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  report.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Even if the report had cited China as a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulator, there would've been no real consequences," Grassley said. "The next step would've been to talk more, and we're already talking."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury said it would continue to pressure China to speed up reforms, but that pressure would be most effective if it were an international effort.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Given our strong disappointment and the importance of China to the world economy, the Treasury Department will closely monitor China's progress in implementing its economic rebalancing strategy, remain fully engaged at every opportunity with China, and continue actively and frankly to press China to quicken the pace of renminbi flexibility," Treasury said, adding that the International Monetary Fund's plans to strengthen exchange rate surveillance should help.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The IMF has repeatedly warned that unbalanced global trade is a serious threat to the world economy. They have said U.S. current account deficit, which reached 6.5% of gross domestic product in the second half last year, is unsustainable and the U.S. is too dependent on foreign borrowing. Should foreigners become unwilling to finance that deficit, the U.S. dollar could plunge and U.S. interest rates could rise sharply - possibly causing an economic recession, economists warn.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Treasury report played down those worries, saying the deficit continues to be financed primarily through private investors, rather than central banks, and neither those banks nor oil exporters show evidence of diversification out of dollars. Treasury also said the large negative international investment position of the U.S. will probably generate outflows on investment income in the near future, but these outflows on investment "aren't likely to be massive."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bush administration argues that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  adjustment and policies to promote faster growth and more investment in other parts of the world would be most effective in rebalancing trade. Japan and Europe should enact reforms to boost demand, Treasury said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Although the euro-area's current account position is near balance, this is in part due to suppressed demand reflecting underemployment of existing resources," Treasury said. "Structural reforms that boost the efficiency of the large services sector would undoubtedly raise European incomes and in turn, imports."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Further investment in Europe has been weak despite very low interest rates, Treasury said. Raising productivity and strengthening capital markets would boost growth and investment and help reduce global trade imbalances, Treasury said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Like China, wider trade balances and or rapidly growing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves were experienced in Malaysia, Japan, Switzerland, Germany, Singapore, Russia and all of the major oil exporting countries, Treasury said. Japan, Switzerland and Germany have freely floating exchange rates and their central banks didn't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets during the second half of 2005.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could look to South Korea for an example of a country heavily dependent on trade, with strong links to the global technology market and closely tied to the Asian manufacturing system, Treasury said. Despite a 9% nominal appreciation of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and slowing demand for its exports, South Korea managed to grow 4% in 2005 and should expand another 5% this year, Treasury said. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation allowed the country to contain inflation and limit interest rate hikes, while domestic demand strengthened, Treasury said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Elizabeth Price, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9295; Elizabeth.Price@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 05-11-06 0733ET  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020060511e25b002qy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\s