ent meetings with Chinese officials at many levels of government. The most likely outcome of such a designation, say analysts, would be to embolden China's critics in Congress who want to put tariffs on Chinese imports in retaliation for its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 Treasury said it would continue to pressure China to speed up reforms, but that pressure would be most effective if it were an international effort.  \par \par \uc2 "Given our strong disappointment and the importance of China to the world economy, the Treasury Department will closely monitor China's progress in implementing its economic rebalancing strategy, remain fully engaged at every opportunity with China, and continue actively and frankly to press China to quicken the pace of renminbi flexibility," Treasury said, adding that the International Monetary Fund's plans to strengthen exchange rate surveillance should help.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF has repeatedly warned that unbalanced global trade is a serious threat to the world economy. They have said U.S. current account deficit, which reached 6.5% of gross domestic product in the second half last year, is unsustainable and the U.S. is too dependent on foreign borrowing. Should foreigners become unwilling to finance that deficit, the U.S. dollar could plunge and U.S. interest rates could rise sharply - possibly causing an economic recession, economists warn.  \par \par \uc2 The Treasury report played down those worries, saying the deficit continues to be financed primarily through private investors, rather than central banks, and neither those banks nor oil exporters show evidence of diversification out of dollars. Treasury also said the large negative international investment position of the U.S. will probably generate outflows on investment income in the near future, but these outflows on investment "aren't likely to be massive."  \par \par \uc2 Even as Americans became more indebted to foreigners, for 2