\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Month-long rally stalls  \b0\par\par\uc2 821 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Mai 2006\par 04:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, May 4 (Reuters) - The Thai baht fell to a two-week low on Thursday while the Singapore dollar, South Korean won and Malaysian ringgit retreated from their highest levels in eight years as traders locked in profits from a one-month rally.  \par \par \uc2 The baht <THB=> and won <KRW=> both lost as much as three-quarters of a percent while the Singapore dollar <SGD=> and Philippine peso <PHP=> lost as much as half a percent.  \par \par \uc2 With Japan and China shut until the end of the week and financial markets in South Korea, Hong Kong and Thailand also closed on Friday, many traders decided to book profits from the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rally.  \par \par \uc2 Expectations of a faster appreciation in the Chinese yuan, fuelled by last week's surprise interest rate increase by China, had helped fuel the gains.  \par \par \uc2 But those expectations were doused by Chinese Vice Finance Minister Li Yong, who said in India on Thursday that China could not move too fast on the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 "China wants to tell everyone, 'don't get carried away by yuan talk'," said Jimmy Koh, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at United Overseas Bank in Singapore.  \par \par \uc2 "While everyone was expecting that China would do something with the yuan, China has not moved at all."  \par \par \uc2 The yuan has gained 1.2 percent since last July's revaluation, when China made the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  2.1 percent stronger overnight and ended the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peg to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 In contrast, the won and Thai baht have both surged more than 8 percent while the Singapore dollar and Malaysian ringgit have gained more than 4 percent over the same period. Koh said he expected trading in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be range-bound in coming weeks unless China moved decisively to let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate at a faster pace.  \par \par \uc2 "Markets would want to see what \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  does after the holidays," he said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  WORRIES  \par \par \uc2 Traders refrained from bidding up Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after seeing widespread dollar buying by Asian central banks on Wednesday aimed at stifling sharp gains in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "Everybody's buying back dollars," said a Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer who said local banks, probably under orders from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , had bid up the dollar in early morning trade.  \par \par \uc2 Asian finance ministers gathered in Hyderabad in India for an Asian Development Bank meeting sounded the alarm on the rapidly strengthening Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 South Korean Finance Minister Han Duck-soo said the won <KRW=> had overshot economic fundamentals and that the firmer \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and high oil prices were the main risks to the economy.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier, Japan's finance minister, Sadakazu Tanigaki, told reporters in Hyderabad that "\b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been moving in a somewhat excessive manner".  \par \par \uc2 He said a statement issued by finance ministers of Group of Seven rich countries last month had been misunderstood by financial markets to mean the G7 favoured a dollar decline. He said any excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements were undesirable.  \par \par \uc2 In Thailand, data released on Thursday showed a $2.6 billion jump in foreign reserves between March 24 and April 28, confirming traders' talk that the Bank of Thailand (BOT) was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stall the baht's gains.  \par \par \uc2 "We see the dollar/baht going slowly higher next week," said a dealer in Bangkok. "The BOT does not want a stronger baht."  \par \par \uc2 The baht weakened to a low of 37.89 per dollar. It has lost ground since hitting a six-year high of 37.40 per dollar on May 1 as soaring crude oil prices and political uncertainty finally took their toll.  \par \par \uc2 The baht climbed 3.6 percent in April and is up 8.9 percent so far this year, making it Asia's second-best performer.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at   0802 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       113.85         113.35       -0.44\par  Sing dlr        1.5795         1.5743       -0.33\par  Taiwan dlr      31.684         31.627       -0.18\par  Korean won      939.50         934.30       -0.55\par  Baht             37.81          37.57       -0.63\par  Peso             51.46          51.33       -0.25\par  Rupiah         8786.00        8775.00       -0.13\par  Rupee            44.97          44.86       -0.23\par  Ringgit         3.6110         3.6080       -0.08 Change so\par far in 2006\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       113.85         117.96       +3.61\par  Sing dlr        1.5795         1.6625       +5.25\par  Taiwan dlr      31.684         32.850       +3.68\par  Korean won      939.50        1011.60       +7.67\par  Baht             37.81          41.11       +8.73\par  Peso             51.46          53.09       +3.17\par  Rupiah         8786.00  