\par\par \b \uc2 WSJA(4/28) China Raises Lending Rate In Bid To Slow Growth  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,091 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 April 2006\par 05:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2006, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING -- China said it will raise bank-lending rates for the first time since 2004, a surprise move aimed at heading off overheating and massive overinvestment in its economy caused by runaway growth in bank loans.  \par \par \uc2 Starting Friday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise benchmark one-year lending rates to 5.85% from 5.58%, making it more expensive for companies to borrow money. Deposit rates remain unchanged.  \par \par \uc2 Economists say that authorities are likely to follow up with further cooling measures, possibly allowing the Chinese yuan to rise more rapidly against the U.S. dollar. A stronger yuan could slow the huge inflows of cash that are creating froth in the economy.  \par \par \uc2 The interest-rate rise of 0.27 percentage point is modest. Nevertheless, it is important because it signals that China increasingly is willing to let markets play a greater role in shaping its economy, rather than relying on directives from the central government. In the past, Chinese officials have turned instinctively to bureaucratic measures to curb growth, before grasping market-oriented levers such as interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 The move instantly hit global stock markets by raising fears that China's growth could stall and dull its ravenous appetite for imports. Shares in European mining, utility and oil companies all sank in early trading.  \par \par \uc2 China, now the world's fourth largest economy, has become the world largest importer of a range of commodities, sucking in everything from copper to steel and aluminum to fuel its rapid economic growth. Some economists surveyed after the announcement of the rate increase late Thursday said fears of a sharp slowdown in growth are overblown, but other market analysts said there is cause for concern.  \par \par \uc2 "With the news that rates are being increased in China, the implication is that there could be some slowdown in demand," said Dave Rinehimer, director of futures research at Citigroup Global Markets. "That has negative implications for commodities in general."  \par \par \uc2 The move reflects growing concern among China's top leaders about overheating in its economy. Those concerns deepened earlier this month after figures showed that its economy grew by a red-hot 10.2% during the first quarter of this year, compared to the same period last year. China's President Hu Jintao himself seemed surprised by the pace of the growth. "We do not want, nor are we pursuing, overrapid economic growth," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The 27 basis point interest rate hike is modest. Nevertheless, many economists had thought that Beijing would stop short of such a move, which adds, albeit marginally, to the financial burden on thousands of debt-laden state enterprises which operate on thin profit margins.  \par \par \uc2 Most economists believed that Beijing would opt instead to drain liquidity out of the banking system by raising the amount of money that banks must place on deposit with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- funds that cannot be lent out. That is now likely the next move, economists say, together with cruder administrative methods. These could include passing instructions to managers of large state-owned banks to lend less.  \par \par \uc2 Qu Hongbin, the senior China economist at HSBC, says the hike is too small to have much impact on lending decisions. But he says it sends a powerful signal to banks to rein in their lending. Banks have already dished out more than half the loans they were expected to extend for the entire year. "They want to give people the impression they are serious," says Mr. Qu.  \par \par \uc2 The Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move is precisely what the International Monetary Fund's economist prescribed in its recent World Economic Outlook, nudging China to raise interest rates to brake excessive investment spending. "The pace of the expansion (in China) is expected to slow modestly this year...as the contribution from external demand falls and the government is assumed to act to slow investment growth (which is particularly needed in sectors facing the prospect of future overcapacity)," the IMF said "The risks to this projection in the near term are on the upside given that without further tightening measures the ample liquidity in the banking system could underpin a rebound in lending and investment."  \par \par \uc2 Runaway lending raises the specter of another bout of overheating similar to the one that began in late 2002 and lasted through early 2004. However, Frank Gong, the senior China economist at JP Morgan, says there are fundamental differences this time around. For a start, inflation growth is now subdued -- less than 1% -- compared with some 5% in the earlier period, meaning that prices are now stable. Back then, inflationary problems were exacerbated by transport and power shortages that pushed up prices. But these bottlenecks have since been alleviated. Beijing has hurriedly constructed power stations, laid new rail tracks and expanded its ports.  \par \par \uc2 Also, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has managed in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  years to curb some of the worst investment excesses concentrated in industries like steel, cement and aluminum. For these reasons, Mr. Gong says he has left unchanged his forecast that the Chinese economy will grow by 9.6% this year. Actual growth last year came in at 9.9%.  \par \par \uc2 Still, there is a common thread to the ills of the economy then and now. Economists say there is too much cash swimming around in the economy as a result of booming exports as well as inflows of foreign investment and speculative "hot money". The problem arises as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys up these dollar inflows in exchange for yuan. The resulting glut of yuan suppresses their value, keeping Chinese exports competitive in dollar terms, but eventually some of the surplus funds end up being lent out by banks.  \par \par \uc2 Some economists argue that China needs to allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen, which could dampen demand for Chinese exports by making them more expensive in dollar terms. At the same time, a strong yuan could slow investment inflows by making Chinese assets dearer.  \par \par \uc2 "The underlying problem is the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says Hong Liang, the China economist at Goldman Sachs. Ms. Hong says she believes that such a small interest rate increase makes it more likely that Beijing will pick up the pace of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation. Combined, she says, these moves could have a more decisive impact. "The exchange rate is the other part of the game," she says.  \par \par \uc2 Beijing appreciated the yuan by 2.1% against the dollar last July, but has since allowed it to float higher by only another 1% or so.  \par \par \uc2 Write to Andrew Browne at andrew.browne@wsj.com  \par \par \uc2 -0-  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020060428e24s002be\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
