\par\par \b \uc2 = WSJ: China To Raise Bk Lending Rates To 5.85% From 5.58%  \b0\par\par\uc2 909 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 April 2006\par 12:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2006, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Apr 27, 2006 (DJCS via Comtex) --  \par \par \uc2 By Andrew Browne  \par \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--China said it will raise bank lending rates for the first time since 2004, a surprise move aimed at heading off overheating and massive overinvestment in its economy caused by runaway growth in bank loans.  \par \par \uc2 Starting Friday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise benchmark one-year lending rates to 5.85% from 5.58%, making it more expensive for companies to borrow money. Deposit rates remain unchanged.  \par \par \uc2 (This story and related background can be found on The Wall Street Journal Web site, WSJ.com.)  \par \par \uc2 Economists say that authorities are likely to follow up with further cooling measures, possibly allowing the Chinese yuan to rise more rapidly against the U.S. dollar. A stronger yuan could slow the huge inflows of cash that are creating froth in the economy.  \par \par \uc2 The move instantly hit global stock markets by raising fears that China's growth could stall and dull its ravenous appetite for imports. Shares in European mining, utility and oil companies all sank in early trading.  \par \par \uc2 China, now the world's fourth largest economy, has become the world's largest importer of a range of commodities, sucking in everything from copper to steel and aluminum to fuel its rapid economic growth. However, economists surveyed after the late Thursday announcement of the interest rate hike generally agree that fears of a sharp slowdown in growth are overblown.  \par \par \uc2 The move reflects growing concern among China's top leaders about overheating in its economy. Those concerns deepened earlier this month after figures showed that its economy grew by a red-hot 10.2% during the first quarter of this year compared to the same period last year. China's President Hu Jintao himself seemed surprised by the pace of the growth. "We do not want, nor are we pursuing, overrapideconomic growth," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The 27 basis point interest rate hike is modest. Nevertheless, many economists had thought that Beijing would stop short of such a move, which adds, albeit marginally, to the financial burden on thousands of debt-laden state enterprises which operate on thin profit margins.  \par \par \uc2 Most economists believed that Beijing would opt instead to drain liquidity out of the banking system by raising the amount of money that banks must place on deposit with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - funds that cannot be lent out. That is now likely the next move, economists say, together with cruder administrative methods. These could include passing instructions to managers of large state-owned banks to lend less.  \par \par \uc2 Qu Hongbin, the senior China economist at HSBC, says the hike is too small to have much impact on lending decisions. But he says it sends a powerful signal to banks to rein in their lending. Banks have already dished out more than half the loans they were expected to extend for the entire year. "They want to give people the impression they are serious," says Qu.  \par \par \uc2 Runaway lending raises the specter of another bout of overheating similar to the one that began in late 2002 and lasted through early 2004. However, Frank Gong, the senior China economist at JP Morgan, says there are fundamental differences this time around. For a start, inflation growth is now subdued - less than 1% - compared with some 5% in the earlier period, meaning that prices are now stable. Back then, inflationary problems were exacerbated by transport and power shortages that pushed up prices. But these bottlenecks have since been alleviated. Beijing has hurriedly constructed power stations, laid new rail tracks and expanded its ports.  \par \par \uc2 Also, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has managed in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  years to curb some of the worst investment excesses concentrated in industries like steel, cement and aluminum. For these reasons, Gong says he has left unchanged his forecast that the Chinese economy will grow by 9.6% this year. Actual growth last year came in at 9.9%.  \par \par \uc2 Still, there is a common thread to the ills of the economy then and now. Economists say there is too much cash swimming around in the economy as a result of booming exports as well as inflows of foreign investment and speculative "hot money." The problem arises as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys up these dollar inflows in exchange for yuan. The resulting glut of yuan suppresses their value, keeping Chinese exports competitive in dollar terms, but eventually some of the surplus funds end up being lent out by banks.  \par \par \uc2 Some economists argue that China needs to allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen, which could damp demand for Chinese exports by making them more expensive in dollar terms. At the same time, a strong yuan could slow investment inflows by making Chinese assets more expensive.  \par \par \uc2 "The underlying problem is the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says Hong Liang, the China economist at Goldman Sachs. Hong says she believes that such a small interest rate increase makes it more likely that Beijing will pick up the pace of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation. Combined, she says, these moves could have a more decisive impact. "The exchange rate is the other part of the game," she says.  \par \par \uc2 Beijing appreciated the yuan by 2.1% against the dollar last July, but has since allowed it to float higher by only another 1% or so.  \par \par \uc2 -By Andrew Browne, The Wall Street Journal; 86 106 588 5848; andrew.browne@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires  \par \par \uc2 04-27-06 1007ET  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020060427e24r0023v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
