\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Yuan resists G7 pressure, drags others down  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan  \par \uc2 910 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 April 2006\par 04:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, April 24 (Reuters) - China's yuan stayed weak for most of Monday, defying a Group of Seven nations' statement singling out the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their call for more flexible exchange rates and reining in the South Korean won, Singapore dollar and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at multi-year highs.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan's <CNY=CFXS> early weakness baffled traders, particularly as the Japanese yen <JPY=> surged to 3-month highs around 115 per dollar, and prompted some to speculate the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the yuan from rising.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed the central point of the yuan's trading band for Monday at 8.0185 per dollar, weaker than Friday's close at 8.0170.  \par \par \uc2 Group of Seven finance ministers and central bankers said in a communique on Friday that "greater exchange rate flexibility is desirable in emerging economies with large current account surpluses, especially China, for necessary adjustments to occur".  \par \par \uc2 In a separate one-page statement on global economic distortions, the officials said more flexibility and a rise in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  were "critical" as was the need for the Asian giant to rely less on exports and more on domestic demand.  \par \par \uc2 People's Bank of China Governor Zhou Xiaochuan later told reporters the yuan could probably begin to rise a bit faster but that gradualism was the guiding philosophy.  \par \par \uc2 "The message is clear. They don't want the market to get carried away with the notion of a rapid appreciation," said Sean Callow, Westpac Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist.  \par \par \uc2 Yet, Zhou's comment was significant, Callow said, and that was why expectations of accelerated gains in the yuan in the future was leading to speculative purchases of Asia's more liquid \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the won and Singapore dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=> briefly hit 1.5890 per U.S. dollar, its strongest since May 1998, and then settled to around 1.5912/16, nearly half a percent firmer than Friday's levels.  \par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> rose to its strongest level since July 2004, while the won <KRW=> was up 1 percent from Friday at around 939 a dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "I still see hot money coming in on the back of stock and bonds purchases. The high yield in rupiah still attracts offshore investors to short term assets," one Jakarta-based trader said of the rupiah's rally.  \par \par \uc2 The Philippine peso <PHP=> continued to buck the regional uptrend. It weakened further to 51.75 per dollar, its weakest in 2 months, and ended local trade off that low.  \par \par \uc2 "There's some month-end corporate demand from oil firms and the manufacturing sector," Amando Tetangco, the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor said, when asked about pressures on the peso.  \par \par \uc2 DOLLAR UNDER PRESSURE  \par \par \uc2 Many analysts also saw the G7's reference to global imbalances as a clear message that the U.S. dollar needed to weaken, more so against the yuan and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 It also meant that the U.S. Treasury's semi-annual report on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  practices of its trading partners was likely to label China a manipulator, they said. "The G7 statement is more than just a message to \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It is a message to all central banks that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  over long periods of time to maintain their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at non-market driven levels," said HSBC's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Richard Yetsenga.  \par \par \uc2 Yetsenga said the Thai baht <THB=> and won were his picks since they were backed by strong economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 Deutsche Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist James Malcolm said the won was likely to be a favourite among investors in Asia because of its liquid markets and its higher volatility.  \par \par \uc2 Yet, he also said, Zhou's comments indicated China was unlikely to respond to the G7 calls and let the yuan rise rapidly.  \par \par \uc2 "It is a medium-term positive for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It is signalling more pressure from the policy perspective," Malcolm said, referring to the G7 statement.  \par \par \uc2 "But as a short-term signal, it is likely to be pretty weak."   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day at 0801 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       115.07         117.60       +2.20\par  Sing dlr        1.5901         1.5976       +0.47\par  Taiwan dlr      31.982         32.303       +1.00\par  Korean won      939.60         948.40       +0.94\par  Baht             37.57          37.85       +0.75\par  Peso             51.64          51.69       +0.11\par  Rupiah         8820.00        8888.00       +0.77\par  Rupee            44.98          45.11       +0.30\par  Ringgit         3.6475         3.6583       +0.30\par  Yuan            8.0155         8.0170       +0.02\par  Change so far in 2006\par  \f28 \b \highlight22