\par\par \b \uc2 China economist sees 9 pct growth in 2006-paper  \b0\par\par\uc2 319 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 April 2006\par 04:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING, April 24 (Reuters) - China's economy will grow at least 9 percent this year while inflation could nudge higher than expected as the government pushes oil price reforms, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist said in remarks published on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 "GDP growth will be at least 9 percent this year while price rises could be higher than 2 percent if oil reforms proceed," the China Securities Journal quoted Jiao Jinpu, deputy head of the research bureau of the People's Bank of China, as saying.  \par \par \uc2 China's annual economic growth accelerated to 10.2 percent in the first three months of this year from 9.9 percent in the previous quarter, strengthening the case for tightening steps, which may include raising required bank reserves and a quicker rise in the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 But annual consumer price inflation slowed to 0.8 percent in March from 0.9 percent in February, extending a downward trend of recent months, government data showed. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has forecast inflation could be around 2 percent this year.  \par \par \uc2 The government has unveiled a series of tax and pricing reforms to better link its oil markets to global benchmarks and encourage energy efficiency to help sustain economic growth.  \par \par \uc2 Separately, the Beijing Unirule Institute of Economics, an independent think-tank, said in a research report it expected the economy to grow about 10 percent in the second quarter.  \par \par \uc2 The think-tank urged the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to widen the yuan's trading band to allow greater \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility to help head off economic overheating, according to the report published in the China Securities Journal.  \par \par \uc2 "Raising bank reserve requirements is feasible. But it will be more meaningful to let residents hoard more \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , reduce market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and widen the floating band," it said.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-\b \highlight22\uc2 CHINA\b0 \highlight\uc2 -GDP|LANGEN|Z|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|ABX|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060424e24o00041\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
