\par\par \b \uc2 DJ US's Zoellick: Hu Should Sell China Trade To US Public  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Price Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 991 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 2006\par 07:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2006, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--A U.S.-China summit meeting this week gives China a chance to convince the American public that U.S. trade with China brings mutual benefits, a senior Bush administration official said Monday.  \par \par \uc2 'For those of us who want to keep America's markets open, we need help from the other side to demonstrate its a fair two-way street,' U.S. Deputy Secretary of State Robert Zoellick said in remarks at the Institute of International Economics. 'Part of President Hu's challenge will be to speak to the U.S. public, not just to officials.'  \par \par \uc2 The meeting between President George W. Bush and Chinese President Hu Jintao Thursday follows meetings on trade last week where China made promises to address some the most intense trade irritants between them. These ranged from eventually lifting a Chinese ban on imports of U.S. beef to putting a timetable on the start of talks for China to adopt World Trade Organization rules for government procurement.  \par \par \uc2 The Bush-Hu meeting will cover issues beyond economics and trade, like how to confront Iran and North Korean nuclear ambitions. Still, China's policy of heavily managing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a source of trade friction left unaddressed last week, will be high on the agenda, Zoellick said.  \par \par \uc2 On \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reform, 'China seems to be saying the right things; it seems to be embedded in its program,' Zoellick said. 'The head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  talks about this in a way that seems we are heading in the right direction but the process of change seems agonizingly slow.'  \par \par \uc2 With mid-term congressional elections approaching and U.S. trade deficits mounting, the Bush administration is under greater pressure from lawmakers to take a tougher line with China on trade issues. A number of legislative proposals are winding through Congress that would impose import tariffs on Chinese goods, or block China's interests in international organizations like the International Monetary Fund and the World Bank in retaliation for its allegedly undervalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Zoellick said the White House is well aware that China is home to millions of poor with a history that makes the government extremely cautious of policy changes although the society is changing so rapidly. There appears to be a 'duality' operating in China with economic policymakers eager to continue the process of reform and global economic integration and the political leadership most concerned about social stability, he said.  \par \par \uc2 But a faster pace of financial market liberalization and a more market-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy could help China meet its goals of job creation and development.  \par \par \uc2 'It doesn't make sense for a developing country to have that large a current account surplus,' Zoellick said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to prevent its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , known as the yuan or renminbi, from appreciating against the U.S. dollar. Chinese officials argue that slow change is best to ensure its weak banking system can adjust without falling into crisis. They also argue that the massive U.S. current account deficit, now above a 6% share of the economy, mainly reflects low U.S. savings, and Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reforms won't have much impact on it anyway. Zoellick disagreed, saying Chinese flexibility would allow China's competitors across Asia to adjust their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well. That would have a significant impact on the U.S. trade deficit, he said.  \par \par \uc2 'When you look at the China \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue, its important to look at its connection to overall Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,' Zoellick said. 'Some of the studies that show modest changes in terms of our current account balance with the change in the renminbi from don't fully account for the fact that this is something that should adjust with Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a whole.'  \par \par \uc2 Zoellick said China needs to take a more prominent role in resolving a range of global problems, because they would reap benefits. He said this is true in talks to persuade North Korea to abandon its nuclear program and in the Doha trade liberalization talks.  \par \par \uc2 China, India and Brazil will likely be among the greatest beneficiaries of trade and globalization over the next two decades, and they should be looking for ways to solve problems in the negotiations to ensure an ambitious Doha round, he said. China has made some very important commitments beyond many developing countries that have been in the WTO a lot longer, but relative to U.S. tariffs, they remain very high, Zoellick said.  \par \par \uc2 'You have to look at these tariff levels compared to the U.S. tariff levels and they are still relatively high in a number of areas at a time when we have a $200 billion bilateral trade deficit and a $700 billion current account deficit,' Zoellick said. 'It's a little hard to sell this to the American public unless it looks like China is willing to liberalize and open its markets further.'  \par \par \uc2 Some of China's reluctance to press North Korea harder reflects a fear of the consequences of a collapse there, he said. Zoellick said China had positioned itself as a mediator more than a participant with strong interests in preventing a nuclear arms race in Asia. Though China has been helpful, the U.S. feels it would be in China's own interests to more forcefully use its influence on North Korea, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Other issues on the agenda include mutual interests in developing non-oil energy sources and promoting efficient consumption, coordination of disaster relief, cooperation in Afghanistan, Sudan and Iraq, and a U.S. desire for China to be more open on its military plans. Zoellick said China would surely bring up Taiwan, although the U.S. policy of supporting the island's current political status hasn't changed. -By Elizabeth Price, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9295; Elizabeth.Price@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020060418e24i002gx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
