\par\par \b \uc2 DATA VIEW: China  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Reserves Rise, M2 Growth Still Fast  \b0\par\par\uc2 566 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 April 2006\par 11:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose to a new high at the end of March, signaling that pressure on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise remains strong, even as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced new steps to boost flexibility in the exchange-rate system.  \par \par \uc2 Furthermore, money supply grew faster than expected in March. As the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  soaks up foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to maintain a stable value of the yuan, it pumps local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back into the economy via bill sales, which pushes up money supply.  \par \par \uc2 The increased money supply could fuel bank lending and make it harder for China to keep hot sectors of its economy under control, economists said.  \par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China Friday said the country added $21.4 billion to its chest in March, bringing its total \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to $875.1 billion, the world's largest stockpile.  \par \par \uc2 China's massive trade surplus and speculative pressure ahead of President Hu Jintao's visit to the U.S. next week are driving China's rapid accumulation of reserves and the rise in M2, the country's widest measure of money supply, said Isaac Meng, economist at BNP Paribas in Beijing.  \par \par \uc2 China's trade surplus widened to a bigger-than-expected $11.19 billion in March as exports boomed, the Ministry of Commerce said this week.  \par \par \uc2 Also, foreign direct investment rose 6.4% in the first three months this year to $14.2 billion, the Ministry said Friday.  \par \par \uc2 In March, China's M2 rose 18.8%, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Friday. This beat expectations for a 18.4% rise and equaled February's rise.  \par \par \uc2 Fast M2 growth spurs banks to make more new loans, leading to overinvestment. A research body under the country's state planning agency said Thursday that fast bank lending was a major obstacle to China implementing its macrocontrol policies to cool overheated sectors.  \par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China set a target for 16% growth in M2 this year, but money growth has surpassed that target in each month so far. New yuan-denominated loans grew to CNY1.26 trillion at the end of March, already reaching half of the full-year PBOC target of CNY2.5 trillion.  \par \par \uc2 "We believe such a loan expansion path clearly falls out of the comfort zone of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Goldman Sachs said in a research note.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    New Steps To Ease Reserve Accumulation \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rapid accumulation of reserves and money supply expansion comes as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  announced new steps Friday to allow individuals and firms to invest more abroad. The move is expected to slow the growth of China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves by unlocking pent-up domestic demand for foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, which topped Japan's earlier this year to become the world's largest, have been a sore point with China's critics abroad. They point to the reserve buildup as evidence that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continues to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to keep its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  artificially cheap, boosting its exports.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The new rules, which allow individuals to buy up to $20,000 of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  a year and raises allowances for companies, will provide more private demand for foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , shifting new foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  holdings away from the government.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Rick Carew, Dow Jones Newswires; 8610 6588-5848; rick.carew@dowjones.com [ 14-04-06 0952GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060414e24e00088\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
