\par\par \b \uc2 DJ China Yuan Ends Higher; Rebounds After Sharp Drop  \b0\par\par\uc2 449 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 April 2006\par 11:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2006, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan closed higher against the U.S. dollar Friday, rebounding a little after dropping sharply Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the U.S. dollar closed at CNY8.0157 on the automatic price matching system after trading in a range of CNY8.0190 and CNY8.0122. Thursday it closed at CNY8.0181.  \par \par \uc2 On the over-the-counter market the dollar was at CNY8.0168 at 0730 GMT after closing at CNY8.0185 Thursday. It traded between CNY8.0190 and CNY8.0136.  \par \par \uc2 The central parity rate for the dollar-yuan was set at 8.0163 Friday.  \par \par \uc2 'The yuan recovered a little after dropping so much yesterday,' said a Guangzhou-based dealer. 'But for the most part, things were fairly stable.'  \par \par \uc2 Thursday, the yuan posted its largest one-day drop against the dollar on the automatic-price matching system since being revalued July 21.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar gained CNY0.0109 against the yuan Thursday after closing Wednesday at CNY8.0072.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the market was unmoved by the launch of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reforms that will allow citizens and companies to make investments overseas for the first time.  \par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China said early Friday banks will be allowed to take institutional and individual funds in yuan and invest them in fixed-income products offshore. Fund management firms and brokerages can take institutional and individual funds in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for overseas investments, including stocks. And insurance companies will be able to increase their investments offshore and buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to invest in fixed income or money market products, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said.  \par \par \uc2 While the reforms may reduce some of the pressure on the yuan to appreciate, analysts said the volume of funds heading offshore in the short term will likely be too small to have a significant impact.  \par \par \uc2 Separately Friday, the China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System & National Interbank Funding Center said an interbank market for yuan-foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps would be formally launched on April 24.  \par \par \uc2 Shen Minggao, China economist at Citigroup, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps will increase the influence of market forces on the exchange rate, and may help ease pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's authorities to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.  \par \par \uc2 The offshore bid/offer quote for one-year dollar-yuan nondeliverable forwards was 7.6690/7.7040, down slightly from 7.7000/7.7050 late Thursday afternoon.  \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong and Singapore markets, the focus of NDF trading, were closed for the Good Friday holiday. Singapore will reopen Monday, but Hong Kong will remain closed until Tuesday. -By Denis McMahon, Dow Jones Newswires; (86-21) 6120-1200; denis.mcmahon&reg;dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by David Riordan  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020060414e24e003mn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
