\par\par \b \uc2 DATA VIEW:Japan  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Reserves Rise To Record High In March  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Natasha Brereton  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 656 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2006\par 07:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    . \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves rose to a new all-time high in March, as the value of the government's euro-denominated assets swelled, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Friday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 But separate data from the International Monetary Fund, based on officially reported figures from the Bank of Japan and the People's Bank of China, indicated that China overtook Japan as the world's largest reserves holder in the first month of the year.  \par \par \uc2 If China did overtake Japan in January, it would be the first time for Japan to lose the top spot since October 1999, according to IMF data.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say China will probably retain that status, since Japan is no longer \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market with the only growth in its reserves coming from changes in the values of and interest on its assets.  \par \par \uc2 "Japan has not been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market ... while \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to allow the (yuan's value) to increase gradually," said Hiroshi Shiraishi, economist at Lehman Brothers in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  fairly aggressively to keep that pace," so its reserves are likely to remain larger than Japan's, he added.  \par \par \uc2 Friday's MOF data showed that Japan's reserves, which include convertible foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , gold and International Monetary Fund special drawing rights, rose $1.97 billion on month to $852.03 billion in March.  \par \par \uc2 That surpassed the previous record high of $851.67 billion set in January and marked the first gain in two months, after a decline of $1.61 billion in February.  \par \par \uc2 An appreciation in the euro against the dollar, which boosted the value of euro-denominated assets held by Japan, was the main factor behind the increase in reserves in March, a government official told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 The euro stood at $1.2116 in New York at the end of March, sharply higher than $1.1921 at the end of February, according to the MOF.  \par \par \uc2 The growth in reserves came in spite of rising U.S. bond yields, which decreased the value of U.S. securities holdings. Bond yields move in the opposite direction from bond prices.  \par \par \uc2 At the end of March, the 10-year Treasury yield was at 4.851% versus 4.555% at the end of February, the official said.  \par \par \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Reserves Overtake Japan's In January  \par \par \uc2 Data from the IMF's International Financial Statistics service, meanwhile, showed that China's foreign reserves including gold stood at $849.70 billion in January, larger than Japan's $839.82 billion.  \par \par \uc2 IMF data differ slightly from those issued by the MOF because of differences in calculation methods.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao also said that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves stood at $853.6 billion at the end of February. Based on Japanese official data, Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves totaled a smaller $850.1 billion at the end of that month.  \par \par \uc2 Replying to journalists questions' on the report, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said it was not possible to confirm at the present time that China's reserves had exceeded Japan's, since official Chinese figures had yet to be released.  \par \par \uc2 "Foreign reserves do not measure national strength. They are a reflection on a country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy," the official added.  \par \par \uc2 "To comment on the size of a nation's reserves would amount to a comment on that country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. The increase in China's reserves is merely the result of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, so I have no particular remarks to make on this subject," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have shown only minor changes since Tokyo stopped its campaign of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March 2004.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Natasha Brereton, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; natasha.brereton@dowjones.com \par    -Edited by Shawn Schroter \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 07-04-06 0520GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060407e2470006x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
