\par\par \b \uc2 China yuan changes to be gradual - govt economist  \b0\par\par\uc2 426 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 2006\par 12:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING, April 6 (Reuters) - China is committed to a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, but the yuan's change will be gradual because the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeps \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a prominent government economist said on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Wu Jinglian, an economist at Development Research Centre, the cabinet's think-tank, reiterated the government's stance of pushing gradual \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reforms while refraining from any drastic action, such as another one-off revaluation of the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 "The trend of the changes will be gradual and smooth and the changes will not be very fast," Wu told an economic forum.  \par \par \uc2 To that end, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, Wu said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was reflected in the country's rapid build-up of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.  \par \par \uc2 "The managed float means the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  still \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the degree of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may change," Wu said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  revalued the yuan <CNY=CFXS> by 2.1 precent against the dollar and shifted to a managed float in a landmark reform last July.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan has appreciated a further 1.3 percent versus the dollar since then and the pace of rises has quickened in recent weeks, ahead of President Hu Jintao's visit to the United States.  \par \par \uc2 But growing \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves had made it harder for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to manage monetary policy and indicated that China was putting too much money earned through trade surplus and foreign investment into low-yielding foreign assets, Wu said.  \par \par \uc2 "It's not the case that the higher the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the better," he said.  \par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves hit $853.6 billion at the end of February, exceeding Japan's for the first time, which stood at $850.1 billion at the end of February.  \par \par \uc2 Wu said China was trying to boost consumption to reduce the reliance on exports, the United States should also take action to cut its trade deficit with China, which hit a record $202 billion in 2005. Washington is calling for faster rises in the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 Chinese officials have argued that the huge trade gap was the outcome of broader economic shifts and urged Washington to raise the U.S. savings rate and also ease curbs on sales of high-tech goods to China.  \par \par \uc2 Wu also called for faster economic and institutional reforms to help limit the government's power in economic activities, which he said was behind the current investment overheating.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-CHINA-YUAN|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|Z|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060406e246001ke\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
