\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-China's yuan falls on dollar strength  \b0\par\par\uc2 515 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2006\par 02:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds market close, dealers  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, March 7 (Reuters) - China's yuan <CNY=CFXS> weakened on Tuesday to close at 8.0424 per dollar, as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened on global markets, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Despite the weakening, the yuan, which was revalued by 2.1 percent to 8.11 to the dollar in July, has appreciated a further 0.84 percent since the groundbreaking policy change. It closed at a post-revaluation high of 8.0365 on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 "The yuan was already under the pressure of a correction after it hit its post-revaluation high yesterday," said a Shanghai-based dealer at a foreign bank.  \par \par \uc2 "Dollar strength on global markets offered a ready excuse for market players to quote the yuan at lower levels to the dollar."  \par \par \uc2 Globally, the dollar steadied against the yen on Tuesday, keeping the previous day's gains as market conviction grew that the dollar's yield advantage would not weaken even if the Bank of Japan ended its ultra-easy monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 It rose some 0.5 percent versus the euro and the Swiss franc, extending gains as U.S. bond yields rose to 21-month highs.  \par \par \uc2 Domestically, dealers said the yuan was still likely to rise steadily, though slowly, against the dollar in the near term because China's heavy trade surpluses and foreign investment have ensured a steady dollar inflow into the market.  \par \par \uc2 "The yuan will quickly recover from its fall today and is likely to rise above 8.0400 tomorrow," said a dealer at a major Chinese state-owned bank.  \par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been the single biggest buyer of dollars on the market over recent years to curb the yuan's gains, but it has greatly reduced \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the yuan since its revaluation, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 There were no clear signs of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in trading on Tuesday, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 On Tuesday \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the daily mid-point of the yuan's exchange rate at 8.0425 against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan can theoretically rise or fall 0.3 percent from its mid-point during the day, though it has only moved a fraction of that range so far in daily trade.  \par \par \uc2 On Tuesday the yuan strengthened to 6.8350 against 100 Japanese yen from Monday's 6.8700, and it firmed to 9.6150 versus the euro from 9.6990.  \par \par \uc2 The exchange-traded market closes at 0730 GMT but over-the-counter trading in the yuan continues until 0930 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 One-year onshore yuan forwards were quoted at 7.8100 to the dollar by 0808 GMT on Tuesday, anticipating the yuan would be 3.0 percent stronger in one year's time.  \par \par \uc2 The onshore forwards market also trades until 0930 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 More information on OTC and onshore forwards trade can be seen on the exchange's Web site ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.chinamoney.com.cn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.chinamoney.com.cn }}}\uc2 ).  \par \par \uc2 To see a guide on contributions, please click <CN/CONT1>.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA-YUAN (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|RBN|Z|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060307e237000ul\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
