\par\par \b \uc2 China Yuan Ends Dn; PBOC Report Dims Yuan Revalue Hopes  \b0\par\par\uc2 390 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Februar 2006\par 09:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI Dow Jones)--China's yuan ended slightly lower Thursday as the market took a breather before it starts an upward trend, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar closed at CNY8.0480 on the automatic price matching system after trading in a range of CNY8.0480 to CNY8.0470. On Wednesday it closed at CNY8.0475.  \par \par \uc2 On the over-the-counter market the dollar was at CNY8.0480 at 0700 GMT. It traded between CNY8.0484 and CNY8.0474. Wednesday, it closed at CNY8.0486, traders said. The central parity rate for the dollar/yuan pair was 8.0490 Thursday, slightly lower than 8.0497 a day earlier.  \par \par \uc2 "The yuan is taking a breather after sharp rises earlier this month. It is gearing up for more gains," said a Shenzhen-based trader. He said he expects the dollar/yuan pair to hit 8.0450 next week.  \par \par \uc2 Between Feb. 6 and Feb. 13, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained at a faster pace than at any time since July's revaluation, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of scaling back from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, to soften U.S. criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 "The yuan may jump again anytime soon. But with the interruption of market corrections, the overall pace of the yuan appreciation will remain slow," said a Shanghai-based trader.  \par \par \uc2 Offshore, the nondeliverable forwards market was quiet Thursday, mirroring trade in the spot market.  \par \par \uc2 Speculation of another one-off yuan revaluation is dying down after China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reiterated its intention in a report this week to keep the yuan basically stable, said a trader based in Singapore.  \par \par \uc2 He said he expects NDFs to drift down in line with spot trading.  \par \par \uc2 The one-year NDFs stood at 7.706 late afternoon, compared with 7.708 a day earlier. The three-month NDFs slid to 7.930 from 7.939.  \par \par \uc2 In other spot trading pairs, the Hong Kong dollar ended up at CNY1.0370 from CNY1.0367; the Japanese yen ended up at CNY6.8480 per 100 yen from CNY6.7820; and the euro ended higher at CNY9.5891 from CNY9.5871.  \par \par \uc2 -Sun Yan contributed to this story; Dow Jones Newswires; 8621 6120-1200; yan.sun@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by David Riordan [ 23-02-06 0834GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1153  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060223e22n000fs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
