\par\par \b \uc2 China Yuan Ends Up Slightly; More Rises Expected Soon  \b0\par\par\uc2 576 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Februar 2006\par 09:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan ended slightly higher Tuesday, maintaining the stable trend of the past week, and traders said they expect the yuan to start its upward movement soon.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar closed at CNY8.0465 on the automatic price matching system after trading in a range of CNY8.0464 to CNY8.0476. On Monday it closed at 8.0475.  \par \par \uc2 On the over-the-counter market the dollar was at CNY8.0472 at 0744 GMT. It traded between CNY8.0485 and CNY8.0472.  \par \par \uc2 "(The dollar/yuan rate) has fallen a bit today, and I expect it to continue falling, but at a moderate pace," said a trader for a major state-owned bank in Beijing, adding that he expected the rate to fall to 8.0460 Wednesday, but no further.  \par \par \uc2 After a rapid rise in late January and early February, the yuan has been trading close to the current rate for about a week. But some traders said the yuan wouldn't start rising rapidly again until the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday afternoon issued its fourth-quarter monetary report in which it confirmed for the first time that it conducted a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap with 10 banks in late November in a transaction worth $6 billion. The PBOC sold dollars and siphoned off CNY48.48 billion from the money market in the transaction.  \par \par \uc2 "As the dollar/yuan interest rate gap becomes bigger, commercial banks want to buy dollars through \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps and invest in dollar assets," the PBOC said.  \par \par \uc2 The monetary report didn't immediately have any effect on the market. One state-owned bank trader said commercial banks won't want to conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps if the swap rate is as high as it was last time, which was CNY7.85/dollar. [ 21-02-06 0830GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the stable onshore rates started pushing the long-end offshore nondeliverable forwards up. They fell sharply Monday morning on rising speculation that China will allow the yuan to rise much faster as President Hu Jintao is scheduled to visit the U.S. in April and as some U.S. politicians increase their criticism of China.  \par \par \uc2 "U.S. lawmakers are becoming more open and aggressive in urging a faster yuan appreciation as the criteria for China not to be named a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator in the next U.S. Treasury \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Report due in April," said Philip Wee, senior market strategist at DBS Bank, in a note Tuesday morning.  \par \par \uc2 Wee said he expects the dollar/yuan to fall marginally below 8.00 around the time of the visit.  \par \par \uc2 The one-year NDFs rose 0.0120 to 7.7040 late afternoon Tuesday. A Hong Kong-based trader said that he heard of Chinese corporates buying them.  \par \par \uc2 The two-month NDFs fell 0.0020 to 7.9660, and another Hong Kong-based trader said it showed continued market jitters about a possible change in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy ahead of Hu's visit.  \par \par \uc2 In other spot trading pairs, the Hong Kong dollar ended down at CNY1.0366 from CNY1.0368; the Japanese yen ended down at CNY6.7860 per 100 yen from CNY6.8150; and the euro ended lower at CNY9.5860 from CNY9.6250.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jane Lanhee Lee, Dow Jones Newswires; (86-21) 6120-1200; jane.lee@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by David Riordan [ 21-02-06 0906GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1153  \par \par \uc2 Dokument 