\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-China's yuan in biggest weekly rise since revaluation  \b0\par\par\uc2 520 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Februar 2006\par 02:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds market close, dealers' comments)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Feb 10 (Reuters) - China's yuan finished on Friday at its highest level to the dollar since its revaluation in July after scoring its biggest weekly gain since then of 0.14 percent, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tolerating its rise, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan closed at 8.0505 per dollar, up from Thursday's 8.0537. It hit new highs against the dollar on four of the five trading days this week.  \par \par \uc2 "The market saw an ample supply of dollars this week, an indication of strong exports and investment," said a dealer at a Chinese commercial bank. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has allowed the yuan to rise without obvious \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the week."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's heavy trade surpluses and foreign investment have ensured steady dollar flows into the market. The People's Bank of China has been the single biggest buyer of dollars on the market over past years to curb the yuan's <CNY=CFXS> gains.  \par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has greatly reduced \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the yuan's 2.1 percent revaluation last July, acting a few times when it believed the yuan was rising too steeply.  \par \par \uc2 On Friday, the yuan saw gains throughout the day after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set its daily mid-point against the dollar, around which it is allowed to fluctuate 0.3 percent each day, at a new post-revaluation high of 8.0516.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said they saw signs that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  encouraged gains in the yuan this week because it allowed stronger mid-points for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 They forecast the yuan would rise above the next target of 8.05 against the dollar early next week after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  broke through the key 8.06 level on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Although the yuan can theoretically rise or fall 0.3 percent from the mid-point during the day, in reality it only moves a fraction of that on most days.  \par \par \uc2 Though the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is allowing the yuan to appreciate gradually, it will still have a strong grip on the pace of its future gains, dealers said. By Friday's close, the yuan had appreciated only 0.74 percent since the July policy change.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan weakened to 6.8400 per 100 yen from Thursday's 6.7880 after the yen surged against the dollar on global markets.  \par \par \uc2 It firmed to 9.6450 to the euro from 9.6470.  \par \par \uc2 The exchange-traded market closes at 0730 GMT, but the yuan's over-the-counter (OTC) trading continues until 0930 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 One-year onshore yuan forwards were traded at 7.8030 to the dollar on Friday, anticipating the yuan would be 3.2 percent stronger in one year's time.  \par \par \uc2 The onshore forwards market also trades until 0930 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 More information on OTC and onshore forwards trade can be seen on the exchange's Web site ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.chinamoney.com.cn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.chinamoney.com.cn }}}\uc2 ).  \par \par \uc2 To see a guide on contributions, please click <CN/CONT1>.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA-YUAN (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Z|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060210e22a0003q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
