\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW: ADB Head: China  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Reserves Rise Big Problem  \b0\par\par\uc2 By James Simms  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 767 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2006\par 08:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The president of the Asian Development Bank said that massive increases in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves still pose a major problem for China, but that reserve accumulation isn't as big an issue for other Asian nations because higher oil import costs have eaten into their current account surpluses.  \par \par \uc2 "Maybe, apart from a few countries like China, too rapid reserve accumulation is not (and) will not be a serious problem anymore. But for China, certainly it still poses a big problem," Haruhiko Kuroda said.  \par \par \uc2 A sharp reserve build-up could hamper "appropriate" and "adequate" monetary policy by flooding the market with funds and also open nations to potentially large \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses, he told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview late Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 "Emerging economies in Asia are well advised to decelerate the accumulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves," he said. "Many of them have more than enough."  \par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, current account surpluses in the Asian region are shrinking and some nations, such as Thailand and India, have fallen into deficits because of the spike in oil prices, Kuroda said. Even those increasing reserves because of factors such as foreign direct investment are seeing the pace of expansion sharply slow, he added.  \par \par \uc2 While China faces a "kind of a bubble" in the real estate market, he said despite the massive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been able to soak up the excess liquidity with bond issues, so-called sterilization of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  action.  \par \par \uc2 By buying dollars to keep the yuan exchange rate stable, the People's Bank of China releases more yuan funds into the money market, bolstering money supply. Unless the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  mops up the excess liquidity via its weekly open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bill issuance, the growing money supply could lead to runaway credit as has been the case in the past, which then fueled unsustainable economic expansion.  \par \par \uc2 "So far, (the PBOC) has been fairly successful," Kuroda said, noting that inflation fell in 2005.  \par \par \uc2 Heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to contain yuan strength helped to push its official reserves to $818.9 billion at the end of 2005. Considering that China's reserves jumped around $50 billion in the October-December quarter, its reserves are widely expected to eclipse Japan's during the first quarter of this year.  \par \par \uc2 Beijing's reserves have swelled as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  absorbed foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  generated by China's growing trade surplus, inflows from foreign direct investment and speculative capital.  \par \par \uc2 In a speech Wednesday, Kuroda, who was the Japanese Finance Ministry's pointman on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues from 1999 to 2003, reiterated that the yuan needed to be more flexible. Chinese authorities abandoned their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peg to the dollar in July 2005 and began referencing a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to determine the yuan's value. [ 09-02-06 0755GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 He also said that small foreign reserve holders could change their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  allocation easily, but that nations with large reserves, such as China and Japan, couldn't because they could trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses. For example, if a major reserve holder started to sell dollar assets to acquire euro assets, then the dollar would depreciate against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 "Large reserve holders have an intrinsic difficulty to diversify," he said. "Large reserve holders are locked in."  \par \par \uc2 Concern last year that Japan and South Korea might alter their reserve allocations caused the dollar to drop.  \par \par \uc2 Moreover, Kuroda said members of the Group of Seven industrialized nations, such as Japan, would have to discuss with the nations of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  involved because it would require \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 "Any exchange market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be subject to prior consultation and any information would be shared among the G7 countries. So, if Japan would like to diversify from the dollar to euro assets, Japan has to consult with the euro as well as the U.S. authorities," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "That could be done if all parties agree. Anyway, I don't think so-called diversification is a big issue or real issue for any of the G7 countries. Even for non-G7 countries, like Saudi Arabia or China, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  diversification is not so easy. But for a G7 member like Japan it's almost impossible," Kuroda said.  \par \par \uc2 Retiring from the ministry in 2003, he was special adviser to the Japanese Cabinet office until taking the top job at the ADB last year.  \par \par \uc2 -By James Simms, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; james.simms@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Chris Gallagher [ 09-02-06 0838GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060209e229000i2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
