\par\par \b \uc2 = INTERVIEW: ADB Head: China  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Reserves Rise Big Problem  \b0\par\par\uc2 495 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2006\par 21:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2006, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Feb 09, 2006 (DJCS via Comtex) --  \par \par \uc2 \f2 By James Simms\par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 The president of the Asian Development Bank said that massive increases in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves still pose a major problem for China, but that reserve accumulation isn't as big an issue for other Asian nations because higher oil import costs have eaten into their current account surpluses.  \par \par \uc2 "Maybe, apart from a few countries like China, too rapid reserve accumulation is not (and) will not be a serious problem anymore. But for China, certainly it still poses a big problem," Haruhiko Kuroda said.  \par \par \uc2 A sharp reserve build-up could hamper "appropriate" and "adequate" monetary policy by flooding the market with funds and also open nations to potentially large \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses, he told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview late Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 "Emerging economies in Asia are well advised to decelerate the accumulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves," he said. "Many of them have more than enough."  \par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, current account surpluses in the Asian region are shrinking and some nations, such as Thailand and India, have fallen into deficits because of the spike in oil prices, Kuroda said. Even those increasing reserves because of factors such as foreign direct investment are seeing the pace of expansion sharply slow, he added.  \par \par \uc2 While China faces a "kind of a bubble" in the real estate market, he said despite the massive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been able to soak up the excess liquidity with bond issues, so-called sterilization of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  action.  \par \par \uc2 By buying dollars to keep the yuan exchange rate stable, the People's Bank of China releases more yuan funds into the money market, bolstering money supply. Unless the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  mops up the excess liquidity via its weekly open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bill issuance, the growing money supply could lead to runaway credit as has been the case in the past, which then fueled unsustainable economic expansion.  \par \par \uc2 "So far, (the PBOC) has been fairly successful," Kuroda said, noting that inflation fell in 2005.  \par \par \uc2 Heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to contain yuan strength helped to push its official reserves to $818.9 billion at the end of 2005. Considering that China's reserves jumped around $50 billion in the October-December quarter, its reserves are widely expected to eclipse Japan's during the first quarter of this year.  \par \par \uc2 Beijing's reserves have swelled as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  absorbed foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  generated by China's growing trade surplus, inflows from foreign direct investment and speculative capital.  \par \par \uc2 In a speech Wednesday, Kuroda, who was the Japanese Finance Ministry's pointman on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues from 1999 to 2003, reiterated that the yuan needed to be more flexible. Chinese authorities abandoned their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peg to the dollar in July 2005 and began referencing a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to determine the yuan's value.  \par \par \uc2 (MORE TO FOLLOW) Dow Jones Newswires  \par \par \uc2 02-09-06 0255ET  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020060209e229000jw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
