\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Money supply growth to spur Chinese market reforms  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Lu Jianxin  \par \uc2 891 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2006\par 04:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Jan 27 (Reuters) - China is scrambling to find new ways to prevent an explosion in its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves from wrecking its efforts to control money growth.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts to add to its traditional tool kit of open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  underline warnings by economists that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  is sacrificing its monetary policy independence by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  massively to hold down the value of the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 The consequences of that policy have been mixed. Despite above-target annual M2 money growth of 17.6 percent in December, consumer prices rose just 1.8 percent in 2005.  \par \par \uc2 But the flood of cash has depressed money market interest rates to less than 2 percent, in an economy whose nominal gross domestic product last year grew 13 percent to 14 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Economists said the strains of coping with this liquidity build-up are growing and reinforce the need to speed up market reforms, such as conducting more yuan swaps and giving more leeway to a newly minted group of yuan market makers.  \par \par \uc2 The economists point to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's record volumes of bills and reverse repurchase agreements last year.  \par \par \uc2 As these deals mature, money drained by them flows back into the banking system, and the bank faces the prospect of ever-higher issuance to keep the money markets on an even keel.  \par \par \uc2 "It's really a matter of ability falling short of wishes," said Yang Hui at CITIC Securities in Beijing. "A huge amount of maturing bills and repos means the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have no choice but to add new tools and make them play a bigger role."  \par \par \uc2 The figures are stark. Over 300 billion yuan ($37 billion) in bills and repos matured in the first three weeks of 2006, meaning the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  managed to drain less than 0.3 yuan for each yuan of new bills sold or repos conducted in the period.  \par \par \uc2 In 2004 and 2005, by contrast, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was able to mop up 0.5 yuan for each yuan in new issues, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 "That means the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has to issue more to get the same proportion of money out of the market in 2006 versus 2005," said an open market dealer at a key Chinese bank. "The situation will become a vicious circle if it continues for years."  \par \par \uc2 REALISTIC  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  mop up, or sterilise, some of the yuan it prints to buy dollars and keep the exchange rate stable -- a big source of excess liquidity in the banking system.  \par \par \uc2 The bank drained a record 3.5 trillion yuan in 2005, up 75 percent from 2004, according to Reuters calculations.  \par \par \uc2 The net drain from the banking system, after deducting maturing bills and repos, was also a record -- 1.34 trillion yuan ($166.2 billion), double the 2004 figure of 669 billion yuan.  \par \par \uc2 That means, roughly, that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sterilised nearly 80 percent of the $209 billion increase in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in 2005, up from around 40 percent in 2004. The reserves now total $818.9 billion, second only to Japan's.  \par \par \uc2 Despite growing difficulties, economists say sterilisation is still more attractive to Beijing than choking off money growth at source by curbing credit or scaling back \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and letting the yuan rise.  \par \par \uc2 A sharp appreciation at a time of widespread industrial overcapacity could usher in deflation, boost unemployment in export industries and saddle banks with new bad loans.  \par \par \uc2 Despite persistent pressure from the United States, Beijing has let the yuan rise just 0.6 percent against the dollar since it revalued by 2.1 percent in July and adopted a managed float.  \par \par \uc2 "China will maintain a realistic approach to decide its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies on the basis of its production, trade and financial safety," said Feng Chen at Galaxy Securities. "Making the yuan fully convertible will only be a long-term goal."  \par \par \uc2 BUYING TIME  \par \par \uc2 Caution also dictates that Beijing is unlikely to raise interest rates or bank reserve requirements to cap money growth.  \par \par \uc2 Instead, economists expect Beijing to gradually refine the way it manages the money markets. It allowed 13 banks to start making a market in the yuan on Jan. 4 and in November began -- with little fanfare and transparency -- biweekly yuan swaps.  \par \par \uc2 Loosening restrictions on the market makers so they can hold more \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  instead of selling it to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would reduce the need for sterilisation. It would be in keeping with Beijing's vow to move gradually to a market-driven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Other reforms under consideration include allowing primary underwriters to buy extra \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills for a day after they are auctioned -- another way of draining cash from the system.  \par \par \uc2 The aim is, in conjunction with an ebbing of hot-money inflows, to contain the impact of the liquidity boom while buying time to rebalance the economy and halt the surge in reserves.  \par \par \uc2 "The status quo will not change much in 2006, though we will see less speculative funds flow into China this year due to the widening gap between U.S. dollar interest rates and those of the yuan," said Wang Haoyu at First Securities in Shenzhen. ($1=8.062 Yuan)  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-CHINA-OPENMARKETS (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|NAT|Z|ABX|BNX  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060130e21r0005h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
