\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-China cenbank says to make better use of yuan band  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Shinichi Kishima  \par \uc2 624 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Januar 2006\par 06:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Releads, adds comments, background)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Jan 25 (Reuters) - China plans to make better use of the yuan's six-month old trading band rather than doing another one-off revaluation, a senior Chinese central banker said on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Yi Gang, an assistant governor of the People's Bank of China, told a seminar in Tokyo that the yuan's 0.3 percent band against the dollar was "quite wide" and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had more room to let the yuan fluctuate within that band.  \par \par \uc2 "We haven't fully used this yet. This flexible mechanism and this managed floating regime will allow us to adjust the renminbi flexibly in the future," he said.  \par \par \uc2 China revalued the yuan by 2.1 percent last July and changed its dollar peg to a managed float against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The yuan is allowed to move 0.3 percent each day against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and by 3 percent versus the euro, yen and others in the basket.  \par \par \uc2 But Beijing has kept the yuan on a tight leash and has rarely let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  move more than a fraction of its theoretical band against the dollar. U.S. and Japanese officials have called for more yuan flexibility to help correct global economic imbalances, reflected in China's $102 billion trade surplus last year and soaring foreign reserves.  \par \par \uc2 China's reserves jumped $209 billion last year to $818.9 billion, making them second only to Japan's.  \par \par \uc2 In order to hold down the yuan, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has had to absorb most of the foreign cash flooding in from the trade surplus, direct investment and speculation.  \par \par \uc2 Yi denied market rumours from earlier this month that China would shift reserves away from the dollar, saying Bejing has diversified over the years with a focus on safety, liquidity and returns.  \par \par \uc2 "Our diversification policy will be stable," he said.  \par \par \uc2 But Yi said the surge in reserves was constraining the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to conduct independent monetary policy and there would be limits to how much further the stock of reserves could rise.  \par \par \uc2 Yi also said exchange rates alone could not solve the problem of global imbalances such as the U.S. trade deficit.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. lawmakers say Beijing is deliberately holding down the yuan to give its exporters an unfair advantage.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers in Shanghai said \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in trading on Monday after the yuan hit a post-revaluation high of 8.0601 to the dollar on Friday in what was the biggest one-day move since the revaluation.  \par \par \uc2 Yi also sought to play down expections among market participants that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also called the renminbi, will appreciate further given its strong economic performance.  \par \par \uc2 Data showed on Wednesday that China's economy grew 9.9 percent in 2005 to $2.23 trillion, leaping over France to become the world's fifth-largest economy and possibly even surpassing fourth-placed Britain, depending on growth rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  changes.  \par \par \uc2 "People believe that there will be further renminbi appreciation, but I would like to take this opportunity to remind you that you have to also look at risks of the Chinese economy," Yi said.  \par \par \uc2 Yi said the Chinese economy still has various problems, including low efficiency in the financial sector, social security liabilities and non-performing loans in the banking sector.  \par \par \uc2 He said such problems implied that the renminbi could be subjected to pressure to depreciate. It has appreciated only 0.6 percent since the July revaluation.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-CHINA-YI (UPDATE 3)|LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|D|M|NAT|U|Z|ABX|BNX|FUN|RNP|DNP|PTD  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060125e21p0009d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
