\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-China yuan rises to new high, mkt sentiment improves  \b0\par\par\uc2 521 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 2006\par 02:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds yuan's last trade, details)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Jan 20 (Reuters) - China's yuan <CNY=CFXS> strengthened to last trade at a record of 8.0601 to the dollar on Friday, its biggest gain in a single trading session since its July revaluation, as traders bet on further appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan gained nearly 0.1 percent in the Friday session after last trading at 8.0673 to the dollar the previous day.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has now appreciated a further 0.62 percent since July 21 when it was revalued by 2.1 percent to 8.11 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan, also known as renminbi, strengthened at the beginning of the week after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves had reached a record $819 billion by the end of 2005, a factor noted by analysts that may help the yuan rise further as Beijing tries to manage its reserves for a better return.  \par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is said to be linked to a basket of major international \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  including Japan's yen, weakened on Tuesday and Wednesday after the dollar gained globally on positive news including stronger monthly U.S. economic data.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't see any special reason to explain why the yuan rose faster today but the broad sentiment on the market indicates more and more investors are betting on further appreciation," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at a European bank in Shanghai.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers noted that investors had also paid attention to comments on the yuan given by Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki and U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow who both reiterated on Thursday and Friday that China should adopt a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 "We don't care what they really say. Words are always the same," said another dealer at a major Chinese bank in Shanghai.  \par \par \uc2 "But one thing is for sure that the international community is adding more pressure for the yuan's rise."  \par \par \uc2 The dealer also noted that the market believed that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Friday trading, which could be construed as evidence that Beijing wanted to let market forces decide whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rises or falls.  \par \par \uc2 On Friday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the daily mid-point of the yuan exchange rate against the dollar at 8.0648 versus Thursday's mid-point of 8.0687.  \par \par \uc2 The rate is part of a system introduced this month as another step towards gradually freeing up \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may rise or fall 0.3 percent from its mid-point rate each day, although in practice before Friday it had never moved more than a small fraction of that figure.  \par \par \uc2 One-year onshore yuan forwards were quoted at 7.877 to the dollar, anticipating the yuan would be 2.3 percent stronger in a year's time. Two deals were done in other durations.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan was last traded against the euro at 9.7450 and against 100 Japanese yen at 6.9600.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA-YUAN (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Z|ABN|M|D|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060120e21k0004i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex10