\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-China approves 13 banks as yuan market-makers  \b0\par\par\uc2 527 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 2005\par 04:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds full list of market makers)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING/SHANGHAI, Dec 30 (Reuters) - China has granted approval for 13 foreign and domestic banks to act as pioneering market-makers for yuan trading, marking another step towards greater flexibility for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , banking sources said.  \par \par \uc2 China said in November it would launch a long-awaited market-making system for yuan trading against foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a move that will start early next year. Such a system, which promotes liquidity, is seen by economists as an important basis for a freer-floating yuan.  \par \par \uc2 Shanghai-based banking sources told Reuters that the 13 banks included the Big Four state-owned commercial lenders, as well as number-five ranked Bank of Communications.  \par \par \uc2 Other domestic lenders included Beijing-based CITIC Bank Co., top Shanghai-listed Merchants Bank Co. Ltd. and Fujian-based Industrial Bank Co.  \par \par \uc2 The foreign banks include Standard Chartered Plc., Citigroup Inc., HSBC Holdings Plc., ABN AMRO and the Bank of Montreal, banking sources said.  \par \par \uc2 Officials from the State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment.  \par \par \uc2 HSBC said in a statement late on Thursday that it had been approved as a market-maker. The world's largest financial services provider Citigroup issued a similar statement. Asia-focused Standard Chartered also confirmed its inclusion on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 "It will enable us to fully participate in the development of the (\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) market, while providing more flexibility for our clients," Richard Stanley, chief executive officer of Citigroup China, said in its statement.  \par \par \uc2 The launch of a market-making system for the yuan is part of a slew of economic and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reforms that has taken place this year, from the creation of a commercial paper market to allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps and forwards on interbank markets.  \par \par \uc2 TIGHT SUPERVISION  \par \par \uc2 Banking sources said that the 13 yuan market-makers could begin their roles as early as Jan. 4, when the market re-opens after the New Year's holiday.  \par \par \uc2 China revalued the yuan by 2.1 percent in July as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  replaced its 11-year-old dollar peg with a system that, in theory, permits the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise or fall by 0.3 percent a day against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Yet the yuan remains tightly supervised by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and has risen just over 0.48 percent since the revaluation.  \par \par \uc2 Critics say the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains undervalued, making Chinese exports artificially cheap, and contributes to a U.S. trade deficit with China that could top $200 billion this year.  \par \par \uc2 Beijing has ruled out another one-off revaluation, executing instead reform measures aimed at gradually allowing market forces to play a bigger role in determining the exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 State media had reported that introducing market makers -- which must stand ready to trade at quoted bid and ask prices -- for the yuan could limit the extent to which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Big Four state lenders are Bank of China, China Construction Bank, Industrial and Commercial Bank of China and Agricultural Bank of China. (Additional reporting by Eadie Chen in Beijing)  \par \par \uc2 FINANCIAL-CHINA-MARKETMAKERS (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051230e1cu00043\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
