\par\par \b \uc2 DJ OECD: Yuan Rise Can Help China  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Rate, Monetary Policies  \b0\par\par\uc2 455 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 2005\par 14:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2005, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--A further appreciation of the yuan would help China resolve problems with its exchange rate and monetary policies and help prevent inflationary pressures from building, the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development said Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 'Increasing stress is likely to emerge in the interaction between the authorities' exchange rate and monetary policies, given the continued size of the current account surplus and capital inflows,' the OECD said in its latest economic outlook.  \par \par \uc2 The OECD projected China's current account surplus to hit $148 billion this year, and continue to expand to $192 billion next year and $202 billion in 2007.  \par \par \uc2 The current account surplus as a percentage of China's gross domestic product will reach 7.8% this year, 8.9% next year and 8.3% in 2007, according to the OECD.  \par \par \uc2 It said monetary policy is being driven in an expansionary direction given the growth of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. The likelihood that administrative controls over bank lending will be bypassed also may lead to faster growth and higher inflation, the OECD said.  \par \par \uc2 Inflationary pressures can be tempered by allowing the yuan to rise further, and will also help China move economic policy toward using market instruments, the Paris-based agency added.  \par \par \uc2 It said a move toward more market-oriented policies will reinforce the progress that has been made on China's banking reform in the past year, such as reducing the need for administrative control of credit.  \par \par \uc2 China's current account surplus in the first six months of 2005 ballooned to $67.26 billion, already matching the $68.66 billion recorded for all of 2004.  \par \par \uc2 Although Beijing said it was moving to a more flexible exchange rate regime when it scrapped an 11-year-old effective dollar peg in July to begin referencing the yuan against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market to keep trading narrow and steady in the main dollar-yuan pair.  \par \par \uc2 At the time Beijing announced the move, it also revalued the yuan 2.1% to the dollar, but the local unit hasn't gained much beyond that since then. By buying dollars to keep the yuan exchange rate stable, the PBOC releases more yuan funds into the money market, bolstering money supply.  \par \par \uc2 Unless the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  mops up the excess liquidity via its weekly open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bill issuance, the growing money supply could lead to runaway credit as has been the case in the past, which then fueled unsustainable economic expansion. -By J.R. Wu, Dow Jones Newswires; (8610) 6588-5848; jr.wu@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by David Riordan  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020051129e1bt007vd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
