\par\par \b \uc2 Graham, Schumer Agree To Delay China  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Vote To Dec  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Price  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 561 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 2005\par 21:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--Sens. Charles Schumer, D-N.Y., and Lindsey Graham, R-S.C., again agreed to delay a vote on legislation that would impose a 27.5% tariff on Chinese-made imports in retaliation for China's tightly managed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime.  \par \par \uc2 Schumer and Graham, speaking on the Senate floor Wednesday, asked for an extension of their right to call for a stand-alone vote on the measure until Dec. 23. If China takes some action toward allowing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen, they could push a vote back to March 31, the senators said.  \par \par \uc2 "We are hopeful the president's trip to China will produce positive results and we are willing, of course, to forestall our agreement until the president returns from his trip," Schumer said.  \par \par \uc2 President George W. Bush, currently on a tour of Asia, is expected to meet with Chinese President Hu Jintao on Sunday.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, the measure attracted favorable votes from 67 senators when it was attached to an unrelated bill. It was an unusual show of support from both Democrats and Republicans. Schumer and Graham agreed to withdraw the amendment in exchange for a promise of a stand-alone vote later this year.  \par \par \uc2 They agreed to delay that vote after a closed-door meeting this summer with U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow and Federal Reserve Board Chairman Alan Greenspan. The senators said Snow and Greenspan convinced them China was poised to make changes in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime.  \par \par \uc2 Shortly afterward, the Chinese government officially ended its policy of pegging the yuan at 8.28 to the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would target a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  instead. In the process, the government allowed the yuan to strengthen about 2% against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The Bush administration, and Schumer and Graham themselves, hailed the move as a good first step that should be followed up with more reform. Private economists and those at the World Bank and International Monetary Fund have said the modest reform is unlikely to have much impact on China's growing trade surpluses or curb the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to prevent further yuan appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 Though Chinese officials have said they intend to move to a flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system for the health of their own economy, they also stress that reform will proceed cautiously to avoid disrupting the economy or the financial system.  \par \par \uc2 "In four months the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has moved about as much as it could be allowed to move in one day," Schumer said. "Sen. Graham and I are disappointed so far."  \par \par \uc2 Treasury officials argue their diplomatic approach is the best hope for persuading China to allow a more market-driven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Snow has called the legislation "misguided" and Federal Reserve Board Chairman Alan Greenspan has said he believes trade protectionism is a serious threat to the global economy.  \par \par \uc2 Both Snow and Greenspan, during a visit to China last month, urged the Chinese government to speed up its timetable for a flexible yuan, both to improve the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to control inflation and to defuse trade protectionist sentiment building around the world against China.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par   -By Elizabeth Price, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9295; Elizabeth.Price@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 16-11-05 2124GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020051116e1bg00112\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
