\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-China's yuan inches higher as Bush visit nears  \b0\par\par\uc2 545 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 2005\par 09:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, dealers' comments)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Nov 10 (Reuters) - China's yuan strengthened against the dollar on Thursday as dealers increasingly expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to allow the yuan to appreciate slightly ahead of a visit to the country by U.S. President George W. Bush.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan <CNY=CFXS>, which was revalued by 2.1 percent on July 21 to 8.11 per dollar, closed at 8.0847 versus 8.0857 on Wednesday. It was the third straight day that the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had edged higher.  \par \par \uc2 "The yuan has shown a steady though slow uptrend," said a Shanghai-based dealer at a foreign bank. "That has sparked increasing expectations that China may allow the yuan to rise slightly in coming days as a goodwill gesture to Bush."  \par \par \uc2 Bush, who is due to visit China from Nov. 19 as part of a four-nation trip to Asia, urged Beijing on Tuesday to do more to allow the yuan to strengthen and said the trade imbalance between the two countries was "bothersome."  \par \par \uc2 He is expected to discuss his hopes for more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility with Chinese President Hu Jintao.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the yuan is now poised to test 8.0840, hit on Oct. 28 as its highest level after the July policy change.  \par \par \uc2 But they ruled out a dramatic jump in the value of the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , expecting it to see firm resistance at 8.0800, as most banks trading on the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market impose self-restraint so as not to push the yuan high enough to spark \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's deputy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief, Xiang Junbo, was quoted on Thursday as saying the country would not again adjust the yuan exchange rate as it did in July, and will instead let market forces play a main role in setting its value.  \par \par \uc2 Chinese officials have repeatedly rejected market speculation of another one-off \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  adjustment even though many economists believe July's revaluation was too modest. The United States has also continued calling for a stronger yuan.  \par \par \uc2 But dealers said expectations for the yuan to appreciate in the longer run still surfaced from time to time, supported by China's strong trade surpluses, which have ensured a steady dollar inflow to the domestic market.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, one-year onshore yuan forwards were quoted at 7.7855 to the dollar on Thursday, anticipating that the yuan would be 3.7 percent stronger in a year's time, up from 3.4 percent on Wednesday and 2.8 percent on Tuesday. Four deals were done on Thursday, though none were for one year in duration.  \par \par \uc2 China's surplus swelled to a record $12 billion last month, up from $7.1 billion a year earlier and rebounding much more strongly than expected from a swoon in September, the customs office said on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 On Thursday the yuan firmed slightly against the euro to close at 9.5036 compared with 9.5091 on Wednesday. It ended stronger to close at 6.8602 per 100 yen from 6.8852, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on its Web site ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.pbc.gov.cn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.pbc.gov.cn }}}\uc2 ).  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA-YUAN (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|Z|D|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051110e1ba000uu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
