\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  Yuan Ends Higher; Ctrl Bank  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Suspected  \b0\par\par\uc2 420 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2005\par 09:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yuan ended slightly higher Wednesday as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to keep the exchange rate stable, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Onshore trading of the U.S. dollar ended at CNY8.0857, down from CNY8.0867 Tuesday. The dollar traded between CNY8.0857 and CNY8.0869 during the session, said a trader based in Shanghai.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to moderate fluctuations," said a money-market official at a non-Chinese bank. "It does not like to see domestic companies taking a hit from any sharp rises or falls in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has fallen since it closed at CNY8.0877 Monday as it tracked its gains on the international markets.  \par \par \uc2 "The yuan is still underpriced. It will keep struggling up for a period," said a trader at a Chinese bank.  \par \par \uc2 However, another trader at a local bank said he expected the dollar to meet some support in the near term as many U.S. companies will buy dollars to remit their earnings in China back to the U.S. He predicted a 8.0840-8.0880 trading range this week.  \par \par \uc2 Offshore, the bid/offer quote for the one-year dollar-yuan non-deliverable forward was 7.767/7.777 late Wednesday afternoon, up from 7.7620/7.772 a day earlier.  \par \par \uc2 But investors are awaiting developments during the visit of U.S. President George W. Bush, who is expected to push China for more flexibility over its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime during his trip to Beijing later this month, a trader in Hong Kong said.  \par \par \uc2 Onshore, Industrial & Commercial Bank of China posted a one-year forward price of 780.39 per 100 dollars on its Web site Wednesday morning, compared with 781.19 Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 There were five dollar/yuan forwards transactions on the onshore market: a 273-day deal at 7.8785, a 16-day deal at 8.0725, a 93-day deal at 8.0235, a 172-day deal at 7.9755 and a 364-day deal at 7.8120, the Chinamoney Web site shows.  \par \par \uc2 In other pairs, the Hong Kong dollar closed at CNY1.0420, down from CNY1.0425; the Japanese yen rose to CNY6.8852 per 100 yen from CNY6.8548; and the euro ended at CNY9.5091, up from CNY9.4641.  \par \par \uc2 -Sun Yan contributed to this story; Dow Jones Newswires; 8621 6120-1200; yan.sun@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Sharon Buan [ 09-11-05 0830GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1153  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020051109e1b9000lo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
