\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-China's yuan backtracks from past day's gains  \b0\par\par\uc2 429 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 2005\par 09:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  (Adds dealers' comments, details)  \par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, Nov 4 (Reuters) - China's yuan <CNY=CFXS> back-pedalled against the dollar on Friday, taking its cue from the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength on global markets, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2  The yuan, which was revalued by 2.1 percent on July 21 to 8.11 per dollar, closed at 8.0856 per dollar versus 8.0841 on Thursday, retreating from the previous day, when it came within a hair's breadth of its highest level since the July revaluation.  \par \par \uc2  It has now appreciated a further 0.3 percent since the July move. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  finished at its post-revaluation high of 8.0840 to the dollar last Friday.  \par \par \uc2  "The yuan is now increasingly reflecting the dollar's trends on global markets as \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  less and less in past weeks," said a Shanghai-based dealer at a foreign bank.  \par \par \uc2  The dollar hit a two-year high against the yen and kept gains versus the euro on Friday after comments from the Federal Reserve chief cemented expectations that U.S. interest rates would climb even higher.  \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has raised rates at 12 consecutive policy-setting meetings since June 2004, lifting the fed funds rate to 4.0 percent from 1.0 percent.  \par \par \uc2  In contrast, China's actively traded seven-day repurchase rate <CN7DRP=CFXS> stands at just 1.105 percent.  \par \par \uc2  "The recent slew of U.S. interest rate raises have made it easier for the (Chinese) \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yuan's rise," said a dealer with the Bank of China, the country's top \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  lender.  \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expected the yuan to edge lower against the dollar next week should the greenback hold steady.  \par \par \uc2  But over the longer term, a gradual and upward movement was inevitable, given China's strong economy and strong capital inflows, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2  The Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmed against the euro to close at 9.6560 on Friday compared with 9.7470 on Thursday, and strengthened to 6.8675 per 100 yen from 6.9122, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on its Web site ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.pbc.gov.cn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.pbc.gov.cn }}}\uc2 ).  \par \par \uc2  One-year onshore yuan forwards were quoted at 7.82 to the dollar on Friday, indicating the market believes the yuan will be 3.3 percent firmer in a year's time.  \par \par \uc2  Three deals were made, but not on one-year forwards.      \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA-YUAN (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|Z|D|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051104e1b4000p8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
47
