\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Sens Hopeful After Snow, Greenspan Talk On China  \b0\par\par\uc2 438 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 November 2005\par 21:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds background)  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Elizabeth Price and John Connor \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--Two U.S. senators who met Wednesday with Federal Reserve Board Chairman Alan Greenspan and Treasury Secretary John Snow on China said in a joint statement Thursday that they "had a very productive meeting."  \par \par \uc2 Sens. Chuck Schumer, D-N.Y., and Lindsey Graham, R-S.C., are in the forefront of lawmakers who have been complaining about Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation and urging the U.S. government to take a tougher stand.  \par \par \uc2 "We are hopeful we will make progress in the near future," the two senators said.  \par \par \uc2 Israel Klein, spokesman for Schumer, declined to comment on what he called a private meeting.  \par \par \uc2 The senators have sponsored legislation that would impose a steep 27.5% tariff on Chinese-made imports in retaliation for China's tightly managed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime. Though Chinese officials have said they intend to move to a flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system for the health of their own economy, they also stress that reform will proceed cautiously to avoid disrupting the economy or the financial system.  \par \par \uc2 After Graham and Schumer held a similar meeting with Snow and Greenspan earlier this summer, they agreed to postpone the legislation, which was supported by a wide bipartisan majority of the Senate, until later this year. At the time, they said Snow and Greenspan had convinced them the Chinese were serious about reform and would take concrete steps toward a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Shortly afterward, the Chinese government officially ended its policy of pegging the yuan at 8.28 to the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would target a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  instead. In the process, the government allowed the yuan to strengthen about 2% against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The Bush administration, and Schumer and Graham themselves, hailed the move as a good first step that should be followed up with more reform. Private economists and those at the World Bank and International Monetary Fund have said the modest reform is unlikely to have much impact on China's growing trade surpluses or curb the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to prevent further yuan appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 Both Snow and Greenspan, during a visit to China last month, urged the Chinese government to speed up its timetable for a flexible yuan, both to improve the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to control inflation and to defuse trade protectionist sentiment building around the world against China.  \par \par \uc2 -By John Connor and Elizabeth Price; Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9273; John.Connor@dowjones.com [ 03-11-05 2043GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020051103e1b3000un\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
