\par\par \b \uc2 US Tsy:China Fincl Reforms As Important As  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Flexibility  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Price  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 864 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 2005\par 12:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 XIANGHE, China (Dow Jones)--U.S. Treasury officials Saturday said they hope to intensify and broaden the debate over China's economic reforms in the coming weeks, putting a strategy for developing domestic demand and financial services on an equal footing with the call for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility.  \par \par \uc2 Deeper financial markets will help lay the groundwork for increased exchange-rate flexibility and should receive equal attention, the U.S. Treasury's top international policymaker told reporters on the sidelines of a meeting of G20 finance ministers and central bankers.  \par \par \uc2 The G20 gathering in Xianghe, a city south of Beijing, comes just ahead of an annual meeting on economic issues between the U.S. and China, known as the U.S.-China Joint Economic Commission.  \par \par \uc2 "If we truly want to deal with these imbalances bilaterally or multilaterally, you need to focus on more than just the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said U.S. Treasury Undersecretary for International Affairs Tim Adams. "What we have tried to do is take a quantum leap in sophistication and scope so that we can with Congress and with others have a much more informed discussion of how we actually get these things done."  \par \par \uc2 In a U.S. statement on priorities for China's financial system development to be delivered to the Chinese during their meetings this weekend and Monday, the U.S. called on China to allow established foreign banks, insurance and securities firms to apply for multiple branches on the same terms that domestic firms do so.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. wants China to remove the $10 billion minimum requirements for assets that restrict the ability of foreign institutional investors to buy domestic securities. The U.S. also wants China to remove the foreign ownership caps on financial institutions and allow 100% foreign ownership of subsidiaries.  \par \par \uc2 For two years, Bush administration officials have pressed China to allow market forces more influence on the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They have argued that a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would ease the burden of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  now required to prevent \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yuan from appreciating, and instead allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to focus more on keeping inflation in check.  \par \par \uc2 Chinese officials often reaffirm their goal of eventually moving to a floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but have stressed the need to move cautiously and gradually in an economy with a weak banking system and hundreds of millions of people living in poverty.  \par \par \uc2 In July, China abandoned its policy of pegging the yuan at 8.28 to the U.S. dollar in favor of managing the yuan value against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In the process, it allowed the yuan to appreciate by just 2% and said it would allow the dollar-yuan rate to float in a trading band of plus or minus 0.3%.  \par \par \uc2 It was enough to persuade more vocal critics in the U.S. Congress to put off legislation that would impose steep tariffs on China's imports. But China's critics in Congress have said the yuan must rise further within the next six months to head off new tariff proposals.  \par \par \uc2 Trade protectionism, particularly aimed at China, is a rising trend around the world. It is a serious threat to the global economy, U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan, International Monetary Fund Managing Director Rodrigo de Rato, and U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow all have said.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. Treasury hopes that by putting more emphasis on China's financial reforms, it can call attention to progress China has made in this area and at the same time push China to open its financial system to more foreign competition.  \par \par \uc2 "It does indeed help us to work with Congress to explain the enormity of the reform effort that needs to be undertaken here and how many of these things work together," Adams said.  \par \par \uc2 On the domestic front, Treasury said it would step up communication by publishing a new series of research papers on economic issues, the first one to be on Chinese economic reform.  \par \par \uc2 It also plans to send a steady stream of Treasury officials and advisers, including some who typically cover domestic policy, to visit China and talk about reforms. For example U.S. Treasury Assistant Secretary for Financial Markets Timothy Bitsburger plans to visit China next week to speak at a conference on bond markets and Undersecretary for Domestic Finance Randal Quarles will travel to Hong Kong in November.  \par \par \uc2 Treasury officials said they had no intention of easing pressure on China to allow more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility and repeated their position that China is now ready to allow more yuan fluctuation.  \par \par \uc2 "They can certainly undertake more flexibility than we have seen; they aren't even testing the band they currently have in place," a Treasury official said on condition he not be named.  \par \par \uc2 Snow, who earlier Saturday discussed "economic issues broadly" for a few minutes with Chinese President Hu Jintao, will continue to discuss the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with an array of Chinese officials, the Treasury official said.  \par \par \uc2 -By Elizabeth Price, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9295; elizabeth.price@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Andrew Bullard [ 15-10-05 1039GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020051015e1af0001l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
