\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-China yuan up vs dlr in listless pre-holiday trade  \b0\par\par\uc2 441 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2005\par 10:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds details)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Sept 30 (Reuters) - China's yuan <CNY=CFXS> firmed a tad to 8.0920 per dollar on Friday after having held steady throughout the session ahead of a week-long break.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan, which was revalued by 2.1 percent on July 21 to 8.11, is now a further 0.22 percent stronger. It closed at 8.0930 on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will be closed all of next week. Trading will resume on Saturday, Oct. 8.  \par \par \uc2 "Banks only conducted emergency business today as settlement of today's deals can only be done after the holiday, according to exchange rules," said a dealer at a major Chinese bank.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said they expected the dollar to strengthen during the holiday break and push the yuan lower once business resumes.  \par \par \uc2 But they said the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to continue appreciating gradually in the longer run, propelled by China's strong trade surplus and foreign investment.  \par \par \uc2 Globally, the dollar hit a two-month high against the British pound and stayed in sight of a two-month peak versus the yen on Friday as views strengthened in the market that U.S interest rates would keep rising.  \par \par \uc2 "Last week's U.S. interest rate rise -- and expectations of further U.S. rate rises -- have eased pressure on the yuan to appreciate to some extent," said a dealer at a foreign bank.  \par \par \uc2 "That's allowed \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reduce its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's swelling trade surpluses have caused continuous heavy inflows of dollars into the domestic market, and dealers predict that potential for the yuan to fall will be limited in the medium term.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been the single biggest dollar buyer for more than two years on the domestic market, helping to stabilise the yuan when it faced upward pressure.  \par \par \uc2 One-year onshore forwards were quoted at 7.8430 to the dollar on Friday, anticipating a 3.1 percent appreciation for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in one-year's time.  \par \par \uc2 One deal each in 25-, 41- and 375-days forwards were done, making Friday one of the most active days for the onshore forwards trading since its launch on Aug. 15.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan strengthened to close at 9.7388 against the euro on Friday versus Thursday's 9.7593 and firmed to end at 7.1529 against 100 Japanese yen from 7.1656, according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.pbc.gov.cn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.pbc.gov.cn }}}\uc2 ).  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA-YUAN (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|Z|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050930e19u000jn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
