\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-China yuan edges up vs dollar, more gains seen  \b0\par\par\uc2 430 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2005\par 10:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, dealers' comments)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Sept 27 (Reuters) - China's yuan <CNY=CFXS> strengthened by a whisker against the dollar on Tuesday as ample supply of the hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in domestic markets offset a firmer dollar globally.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan, which was revalued by 2.1 percent on July 21 to 8.11, closed at 8.0920 to the dollar, versus 8.0929 on Monday. It has strengthened by 0.22 percent since the revaluation.  \par \par \uc2 "Dollar supply was very strong" on the domestic market, said a trader at a major Chinese bank. "But a strengthening dollar on global markets made dealers wary about selling dollars heavily."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar surged across the board on Tuesday, bolstered by hawkish comments from Federal Reserve officials that U.S. monetary policy needed to be tightened further to keep inflation at bay.  \par \par \uc2 China's swelling trade surpluses have caused continuous heavy inflows of dollars into the domestic market. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been the single biggest dollar buyer for more than two years, helping stabilise the yuan when it faced upward pressure.  \par \par \uc2 Beijing on Tuesday reinforced its message that it was gradually working towards a more flexible exchange rate, with state media carrying comments from central bankers who also stressed the need to take it one step at a time.  \par \par \uc2 Hu Xiaolian, deputy governor of the People's Bank of China, repeated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's mantra that the yuan would stay at a "reasonable and balanced" level, the Financial News said.  \par \par \uc2 Still, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tolerated the yuan's mild gains on Tuesday, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan could appreciate steadily to hit 8.0700 per dollar in six to eight weeks -- doubling the amount of appreciation seen since the revaluation, they predicted.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said slowing growth in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves pointed to less \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the revaluation.  \par \par \uc2 The country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves had risen by more than $10 billion in August, Zheng Xinli, deputy head of the party's Central Policy Research Office, said last week.  \par \par \uc2 That compared with a jump of $29 billion in July to more than $740 billion, the world's largest stockpile after Japan's, Tang Xu, head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's research bureau, said on Sept. 16.  \par \par \uc2 One-year onshore forwards were quoted at 7.8429 to the dollar on Tuesday, anticipating a 3.1 percent appreciation for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in one-year's time.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA-YUAN (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|Z|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050927e19r000ts\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
