\par\par \b \uc2 New China  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  limits help yuan control-economist  \b0\par\par\uc2 576 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2005\par 11:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Sept 27 (Reuters) - China's step to let companies and banks hold more \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  will help the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cap the yuan's appreciation with less market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , an influential Chinese economist said on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "Allowing more \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  holding by firms and bigger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions by banks will reduce sales on the interbank market and in turn give the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  more leeway in deciding whether to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Ba Shusong, vice head of the financial research institute at the cabinet's Development Research Centre.  \par \par \uc2 Last week China announced new rules allowing banks to run bigger foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions, a step aimed at easing upward pressure on the yuan while and creating conditions towards more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility.  \par \par \uc2 The specific limits were not published.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  must buy any foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that a bank holds in excess of the maximum, so the relaxed limits ease upward pressure on the official reserves, already the world's second largest.  \par \par \uc2 As an adjunct to the cabinet, the Development Research Centre is one of the leading influences on Chinese government policy.  \par \par \uc2 Ba said he believed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which last week widened the yuan's trading band against non-dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , may eventually widen the yuan's tight trading band against the U.S. dollar, but such a move was unnecessary in the near future.  \par \par \uc2 "The trading band is considered sufficient at the moment, but widening it should be the long-term trend," he told Reuters on the sidelines of a financial conference.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Sunday that the yuan/dollar trading band of 0.3 percent a day was appropriate, underlining its reluctance to free up the rate despite foreign pressure to do so.  \par \par \uc2 Economists interpreted the move to widen the yuan's daily trading band against non-dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 3.0 percent from 1.5 percent as acknowledging demand from Washington for greater flexibility while keeping the yuan on a tight leash against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Ba said that to keep the yuan basically stable China must boost domestic demand, tilting growth away from over-reliance on exports.  \par \par \uc2 "We must push economic adjustments to turn domestic demand into a growth engine if we want to keep the yuan relatively stable while pushing forward reforms of the exchange rate regime," Ba said.  \par \par \uc2 To achieve that end, the government must beef up efforts to boost consumption and relax controls on private investment in infrastructure and services, such as medical care, Ba said.  \par \par \uc2 China chalked up a trade surplus of $60.2 billion in the first eight months of the year, and economists expect the figure for the whole year to be triple the 2004 total of $32 billion.  \par \par \uc2 The surging trade surplus is evidence for many economists that the yuan remains too cheap despite a landmark 2.1 percent revaluation on July 21, when Beijing scrapped a long-held dollar peg and adopted a managed float for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Remarks by top Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials published in state media on Tuesday reinforced Beijing's policy that it was gradually working towards a more flexible exchange rate regime.  \par \par \uc2 Hu Xiaolian, deputy governor of the People's Bank of China, repeated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's mantra that the yuan would stay at a "reasonable and balanced" level, the Financial News said.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-\b \highlight22\uc2 CHINA\b0 \highlight\uc2 -YUAN-\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 |LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|Z|ABX|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050927e19r000qt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
