\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan says latest yuan tweak not vital change  \b0\par\par\uc2 482 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2005\par 15:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates with more comments, background)  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Sept 23 (Reuters) - A widening of the Chinese yuan's trading band against non-dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday is a technical move that does not affect China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki's remarks were in line with perceptions in financial markets as China's announcement -- which came just hours before a meeting of Group of Seven (G7) finance chiefs in Washington -- showed the yuan's tight trading band against the dollar under its two-month-old managed float system was not changed.  \par \par \uc2 "It's a rather technical issue and not necessarily a vital matter on what they do with the overall system," Tanigaki told reporters after a bilateral meeting with U.S. Treasury John Snow on the sidelines of the G7 meeting.  \par \par \uc2 Asked if he thought the move represented progress in making the yuan more flexible, Tanigaki said: "My impression is that it's more a streamlining of technical issues, rather than a progress."  \par \par \uc2 Earlier, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would now let the yuan <CNY=CFXS> range as much as 3.0 percent a day against non-dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the yen and the euro instead of 1.5 percent. However, it kept the trading limit against the dollar at 0.3 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan has barely risen 0.3 percent in the entire two months since July 21, when China dropped a decade-old dollar peg in favour of a managed float referenced to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its major trade partners.  \par \par \uc2 Because the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs, such as euro/dollar <EUR=> or dollar/yen <JPY=> can easily move more than 1.5 percent a day, speculation has been rife in the market that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in those major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it were to maintain the yuan's trading band against all \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Asked if he thought the latest announcement stemmed from China's intention to keep the yuan on a tight leash against the dollar, Tanigaki repeated that he thought it was a technical issue.  \par \par \uc2 "What this means in the bigger picture of how they manage their exchange rate system remains to be seen, and we'll have to monitor what they do," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki said he did not take up the latest trading band change in his discussions with Snow, which covered a wide range of issues including the economic impact of higher oil prices and Japan's structural reform policy.  \par \par \uc2 On oil, the two ministers discussed how global authorities could encourage more investment in production capacity, and Tanigaki also expressed concern about the economic impact on Asian economies, which rely heavily on Middle East crude oil.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-GROUP-JAPAN (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|U|MNI|UKI|CANT|J|ABX|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050923e19n001bh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
