\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-China  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  move technical, to appease G7-analysts  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Hunt  \par \uc2 694 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2005\par 16:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Sept 23 (Reuters) - China's widening of yuan trading bands against non-dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday was largely a technical adjustment designed to placate governments pressing for more yuan appreciation, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts said on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The move appeared to reduce a potential need for large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by Beijing as well as appeasing major trading partners before a meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers in Washington later on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The widening was likely to have only a fleeting impact on exchange rates of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they said.  \par \par \uc2 "We have the G7 today and I think the China move is a way to respond to possible political pressure," said Sebastien Barbe, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Calyon in Paris. "There won't be any long-term impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market from this."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders initially dumped the dollar and bought the yen on expectations that the adjustment would allow the yuan to appreciate, a move expected to boost other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 But the dollar quickly recovered as analysts concluded it was not a full-scale policy change, as did Japan's Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki, who said that the band widening was a technical move that does not affect China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime.  \par \par \uc2 Markets are speculating whether the G7 will tone down the language of its communique, which for several years has called for flexibility in emerging Asian exchange rates, following China's landmark revaluation of the yuan on July 21.  \par \par \uc2 As well as revaluing by 2.1 percent, Beijing abandoned a dollar peg for a managed float of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in reference to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  which includes the yen and the euro.  \par \par \uc2 China's finance minister and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor will attend a working lunch with G7 officials on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The United States thinks China keeps its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  artificially cheap to boost exports -- a factor behind the huge U.S. trade and current account deficits that sent the dollar to record lows at the end of 2004.  \par \par \uc2 A U.S. Treasury spokesman described the latest move as a technical step in the flexibility process, adding that Washington was continuing to monitor China's efforts to reform its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime.  \par \par \uc2 BAND ON THE RUN  \par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  widened the daily permitted trading band for the yuan <CNY=CFXS> against non-dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 3.0 percent from 1.5 percent. The yuan is still only allowed to fluctuate in a thin 0.3 percent band against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 In theory, the previous trading bands allowed only for a 1.8 percent move in the euro/dollar <EUR=> exchange rate on any given day -- a not uncommon event in the fast-moving \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 The tweaking will give China more leeway in time of sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements and help Beijing avoid taking on the might of the $1.9 trillion-a-day \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market through large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "Greater flexibility in the crosses reinforces \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to manage the dollar/renminbi. This is still a dollar-based \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime," said Derek Halpenny, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Tokyo Mitsubishi. "This means that there is going to have to be a bigger move in euro/dollar in a day -- of 3.3 percent -- before \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  is forced into \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move could also slow the pace of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve accumulation as \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will no longer need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as often to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  wedged in such a narrow band.  \par \par \uc2 China has the world's second largest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which rose by $29 billion in July to more than $740 billion.  \par \par \uc2 "They will have to buy less euro, yen and Hong Kong dollar and that would be negative for those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but only at the margin," said Adam Myers, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS.  \par \par \uc2 "The weighting of the dollar is so big, all other smaller \b \highlight22\uc2 currency operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  get engulfed," said Myers, who estimates the dollar makes up 85 percent to 90 percent of China's basket.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2 -CHINA|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|Z|E|RBN|NAT  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050923e19n0019s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
