\par\par \b \uc2 China cenbank says to gradually lessen  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  role  \b0\par\par\uc2 462 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2005\par 22:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Sept 22 (Reuters) - China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will "gradually" lessen the degree to which it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, but is not certain the country's yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is undervalued, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said in an interview published on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "Gradually the (People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) will carry out fewer and fewer \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and let the market decide," Hu Xiaolian, deputy governor at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , told the publication "Emerging Markets."  \par \par \uc2 "The frequency and level of such \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be gradually decreased," she said.  \par \par \uc2 But Hu, who is also China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief, added: "We think it's still an open question as to whether the (yuan) exchange rate is undervalued."  \par \par \uc2 In July, China ditched a decade-old policy of pegging the yuan to the U.S. dollar, revalued it by 2.1 percent and moved instead to a managed float.  \par \par \uc2 The move has been welcomed by the United States as a welcome first step toward greater \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility. But the United States, which has argued an undervalued yuan gives China an unfair advantage in world markets, has said further steps are needed.  \par \par \uc2 The topic is expected to be on the table on Friday when officials from China and fellow emerging markets Brazil, Russia, India and South Africa sit down with central bankers and finance ministers from the rich Group of Seven countries for a working luncheon ahead of a formal G7 gathering.  \par \par \uc2 The G7 -- the United States, Britain, Canada, France, Germany, Italy and Japan -- has called for greater \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility in recent post-meeting communiques and faces a delicate task in deciding how to treat the issue in the wake of China's July 21 move.  \par \par \uc2 The revaluation took the yuan from 8.28 to the dollar to 8.11. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has moved up only a further 0.27 percent in the two months since the revaluation, a sign the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is keeping it on a tight leash.  \par \par \uc2 "We can't expect the move will change the activity or strategy of the (\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market overnight," Hu told the publication.  \par \par \uc2 She said China still needed to take steps to ensure speculative inflows do not destabilize the economy.  \par \par \uc2 "We should first further develop our capital markets and other domestic institutions, to better use our domestic market to finance business," she said. "We have to implement all kinds of control on this hot money. We have to keep our watch on capital inflows."  \par \par \uc2 "We've repeated this many times: a stable exchange rate is in China's best interest," Hu said.  \par \par \uc2 GROUP-CHINA-YUAN|LANGEN|ABN|D|M|E|RBN|U|J|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050922e19m002a4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
