\par\par \b \uc2 China Yuan Ends Lower After Five Sessions Of Gains  \b0\par\par\uc2 484 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2005\par 10:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan ended lower Monday after five straight sessions of gains.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  used the U.S. dollar's gains offshore as an excuse to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.  \par \par \uc2 Onshore, the U.S. dollar ended at CNY8.0908, after trading between CNY8.0918 and CNY8.0880. It closed at CNY8.0871 Friday. [ 19-09-05 0818GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in to weaken the yuan, and the (USD/CNY rate) is likely to stay close to 8.09 tomorrow as well," said a Shanghai-based trader for a foreign bank.  \par \par \uc2 Other traders also said that dollar gains offshore made it easier for the People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, as the yuan's exchange rate references a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, comments in a local newspaper by a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official warning speculators that they may lose out had little impact on the markets on or offshore.  \par \par \uc2 "The Libor rate is about 4%, while the yield on the domestic money market is about 1.3%. (So) even if the yuan went up 3% one year from now on, one who exchanges large amounts of dollars for yuan would lose money," Yi Gang, assistant to the governor of the People's Bank of China, said at a conference Sunday in Beijing, according to the official Securities Times.  \par \par \uc2 On the offshore nondeliverable forwards market, the offshore bid/offer quote for one-year dollar/yuan nondeliverable forwards was 7.8425/7.8525 up from 7.8370/7.8450 late Friday.  \par \par \uc2 "There's a dearth of fresh news. We're hearing the same old stuff. Local authorities are talking stability, and the U.S. is stepping up pressure for more yuan moves," said Simon Flint, head of emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Merrill Lynch. "If the spot market moves that could generate some interest," he added.  \par \par \uc2 Flint said he expects political pressure to build for a further yuan appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 A G7 meeting of finance ministers and central bankers will be held Friday in Washington, and a U.S.-China Joint Economic Committee meeting next month will include Federal Reserve Board Chairman Alan Greenspan, Treasury Secretary John Snow, and Securities and Exchange Commission Chairman Christopher Cox, according to individuals familiar with the matter.  \par \par \uc2 The annual U.S.-China meeting brings together bank and securities regulators from the U.S. and China, but usually is attended by senior staffers, not agency heads and cabinet officials.  \par \par \uc2 In other pairs, the Hong Kong dollar closed flat at CNY1.0420; the Japanese yen fell to CNY7.2548 per 100 yen from CNY7.3101; and the euro ended at CNY9.8181, down from CNY9.9298.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jane Lanhee Lee, Dow Jones Newswires; (86-21) 6218-3268; jane.lee@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Sharon Buan and David Riordan [ 19-09-05 0912GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1153  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050919e19j000aa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
