\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-China's  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  regime poses fresh puzzle  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Dhara Ranasinghe  \par \uc2 967 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 2005\par 09:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Sept 13 (Reuters) - Taken literally, the rules of China's new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime could mean the country has more than the yuan to keep in line -- its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be forced to try to corral the dollar, euro and yen.  \par \par \uc2 The rules set limits for the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against all of those units, which in theory translates to limits for the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against each other. In other words, China could be forced to battle the nearly $2 trillion-a-day global \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to keep dollar/euro from jumping around.  \par \par \uc2 Or not, as the case may be.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say that this puzzling aspect of China's new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime should not be taken at face value.  \par \par \uc2 Large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make it work is considered highly unlikely. Analysts say the more probable result is that, in the event of volatile markets, China could simply ignore some of its limits and focus on the dollar/yuan exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 "If \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  were to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  it would be unsustainable. It would be very difficult for them to take on the global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in any particular cross that is not their own," said Simon Flint, head of emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Merrill Lynch in Singapore.  \par \par \uc2 "Can you imagine dealing in euro/dollar against the major hedge funds of the world and hoping to achieve a consistent ability to stabilise the euro against your \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and at the same time stabilise the dollar against your \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?"  \par \par \uc2 China manages the yuan <CNY=CFXS> by reference to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its main trading partners, having abandoned the yuan's peg to the dollar in July.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can move up to 0.3 percent against the dollar on a single day and up to 1.5 percent against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the basket, which include the euro, yen and South Korean won. In theory, that means euro/dollar can move only 1.8 percent -- 0.3 plus 1.5 percent -- on a given day.  \par \par \uc2 But some days, euro/dollar moves more than that. It is a problem that, statistically speaking, will come up repeatedly.  \par \par \uc2 Since January 2003 the yen has moved by more than 1.5 percent against a dollar-pegged yuan on 5 percent of trading days, while the euro has moved by more than this amount on 6 percent of days, according to ABN AMRO.  \par \par \uc2 TOUGH CHOICES  \par \par \uc2 Trying to enforce those limits strictly would leave China with some unpalatable choices, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 Here is why:  \par \par \uc2 Say euro/dollar <EUR=>, now trading near $1.2300, jumped 2.5 percent to $1.2608. The yuan closed on Monday at 8.0924 per dollar, so its limit for today is 0.3 percent, or 8.0681. With the dollar so weak, the yuan would quickly rise to its limit.  \par \par \uc2 But the yuan would need to be able to fall against the surging euro too. Euro/yuan <CNYX=CFXS> finished on Monday at 9.9669 and its 1.5 percent limit is at 10.1164.  \par \par \uc2 If the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  honoured those levels and conducted transactions at them, a bank could buy a euro for $1.2539 by selling dollars for yuan to China and using the yuan to buy euros. That would be cheaper than the market rate. In other words, China would be providing a free arbitrage.  \par \par \uc2 Some of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's other options?  \par \par \uc2 -- It could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to get euro/dollar back within its 1.8 percent limit.  \par \par \uc2 -- It could cease yuan trading until markets stabilised, which could throw up fresh problems.  \par \par \uc2 -- Or it could ignore the 1.5 percent limit for euro/yuan, let that pair go further, and ensure dollar/yuan moves less than 0.3 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Many doubt China, even with the second biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in the world, would take on the $1.9 trillion a day global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. China's reserves are $711 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Flint at Merrill Lynch also noted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be politically unacceptable and would introduce inflexibility into the market even as China is under pressure to become more flexible.  \par \par \uc2 Equally unlikely is the prospect of using China to arbitrage major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the basket moved enough to make yuan rates out of synch with global markets.  \par \par \uc2 "The thing with trading in China, is that it is very illiquid. If I were to place a bid order in the onshore system, matched by an offer in the offshore market, by the time it gets filled, the offshore market could have moved against me," said Deutsche Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Mirza Baig.  \par \par \uc2 IGNORANCE IS BLISS  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say China is most likely to ignore any move in the yuan against non-dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  outside the band limits, especially if the move is just over 1.5 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "I think they will see if they can get away with it. As long as it is just outside the band they will ignore it," said Sabrina Jacobs, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist, Asia, at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein.  \par \par \uc2 Ultimately, widening the trading bands would help, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 "China may come out some point and say they allow a wider range, which would be more sensible," said Jacobs.  \par \par \uc2 "Dollar/yuan is what matters to China. Euro/yuan is a product of what is happening elsewhere. After all, they haven't cared about euro/yuan when they had the peg, so why care now?"  \par \par \uc2 The yuan has been allowed to move only marginally against the dollar since it was revalued 2.1 percent to 8.11 on July 21. ((Editing by Adam Cox, Reuters Messaging: dhara.ranasinghe.reuters.com@reuters.net, +65 6870 3840))  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-YUAN-TRADE (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|Z|U|NAT  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050913e19d000i1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
