\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  PBOC:  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Reform Steady, Less  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 792 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2005\par 13:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief during the weekend said the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reform would progress steadily, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  less in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, but it would still take some time.   \par \par \uc2 The remarks come just days before China President Hu Jintao and U.S. President George W. Bush meet in New York where \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues are likely to be one of the key topics of discussion in Sino-U.S. talks.   \par \par \uc2 At the same time, Zhou Xiaochuan, governor of the People's Bank of China, said China does not wish to see fluctuations in world financial markets "caused by our active composition adjustment of the foreign reserves."   \par \par \uc2 "We will not take any imprudent actions in this regard..." Zhou said. China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are the second largest in the world after Japan and stood at $711 billion at the end of June. Much of it is invested in U.S. Treasurys.   \par \par \uc2 China's reform of its exchange rate regime is intended to ease external trade imbalances, boost domestic demand, and raise competitiveness of local firms, Zhou said.   \par \par \uc2 "When a new system has been put in place, people need some time to learn and adapt to the change," he said.   \par \par \uc2 China's managed floating exchange rate regime allows the PBOC "to act as a filter, doing nothing with the normal fluctuations, but smoothing out the abnormal movements including excessively large or too-frequent changes (in the exchange rate)," Zhou said in an interview with the PBOC backed-Financial News on the sidelines of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  conference in Canada. His remarks were posted on the PBOC Web site.   \par \par \uc2 "Such a role of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is adaptive in nature, and may be reduced over time, as the economy gets more resilient to shocks," Zhou said, adding the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would reduce its purchases of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market and more \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be kept in the hands of companies.   \par \par \uc2 Zhou reiterated the PBOC would keep to a gradualist approach as it pushes ahead on its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reform, but the preconditions for adopting a freely floating exchange rate regime have not been met.   \par \par \uc2 China is not well prepared to deal with the possibility of any "sharp and disorderly" adjustments in the global economy, he said.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Insur Funds OK'd For Overseas \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Investments \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Zhou said China's macroeconomic environment is conducive to exchange rate reform work, which is being done to suit the nation's own needs.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He said China's consumer price index growth is moderate, and that rising interest rates in the U.S. were "an important condition" for yuan exchange rate reform.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On July 21, China scrapped its 11-year-old policy of effectively pegging the yuan to the dollar. Instead it revalued the yuan 2.1% against the dollar and started referencing its value to a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In recent days, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials have given cautiously optimistic assessments of the July move, but suggested a further official revaluation of the yuan is unlikely in coming months and that the role of market supply and demand is an important factor in setting the exchange rate.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the spot market, the U.S. dollar ended at CNY8.0956 Friday, up from CNY8.0945 Thursday. The dollar reached CNY8.0912 on Sept. 6, its lowest since the July move, and has been rising since.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts say the degree of revaluation is not enough to have any effect on China's ballooning current account surplus, namely in trade. Critics, especially those in the U.S., have argued the yuan is undervalued and China should do more to allow it to appreciate.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China wants equilibrium in its balance of payments, and statistics in this regard could be improved, said Zhou suggesting a possible revision in the calculation for current account that would be composed mainly of transactions of trade in goods and services.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He noted that at present the current account also includes items for proceeds and current transfers, which are not stable and can be distorted by changes in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation expectations.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, to complement the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reform work, China's top insurance regulator also issued guidelines during the weekend allowing insurance funds to put money in overseas \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  investments.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Participants entrusted with insurance funds will be able to conditionally invest in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  products, overseas shares of Chinese companies, and bonds issued by foreign governments, including mortgage-backed securities guaranteed by the government, as well as money market funds and structured deposits, the China Insurance Regulatory Commission said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By J.R. Wu and Rick Carew, Dow Jones Newswires; 8610 6588-5848; jr.wu@dowjones.com [ 11-09-05 1137GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050911e19b0001s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
