\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan says too early to demand more yuan steps  \b0\par\par\uc2 713 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2005\par 12:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds Weber comment, background)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 8 (Reuters) - The Chinese yuan has moved very little even after Beijing scrapped its decade-old dollar peg system in July, but it is still too early to demand further steps, Japan's top financial diplomat said on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 The remarks by Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Hiroshi Watanabe chimed with later comments from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governing Council member Axel Weber, suggesting the Group of Seven industrial countries would not take a hard-line stance on China at their upcoming meeting.  \par \par \uc2 The G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs, who meet in Washington on Sept. 23-24, may revise their statement on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility to reflect China's reform but must be cautious to avoid unwanted speculation, said Watanabe, Japan's representative in preparations for the G7 gathering.  \par \par \uc2 "We have been thinking the first month or two would be a warm-up period anyway," he told reporters, responding to questions about the small trading band the yuan has remained in against the dollar even after the July policy change.  \par \par \uc2 "We're not in a situation where we call for a next step, and the important thing now is how to implement the new system in accordance with the spirit of the reform."  \par \par \uc2 ECB's Weber, who is also president of the German Bundesbank, told Reuters meanwhile in London that the yuan issue needed careful handling.  \par \par \uc2 "The regime now can allow for more flexibility and it's basically up to the Chinese authorities to use this flexibility in their monetary policy environment," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "The transition from one regime to another needs to be done carefully considering all possible effects."  \par \par \uc2 SMALL FIRST STEP  \par \par \uc2 China revalued the yuan by 2.1 percent against the dollar and scrapped its peg to the U.S. dollar in favour of a managed float system referenced to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its major trade partners on July 21.  \par \par \uc2 Since then, it has risen barely 0.2 percent, closing at 8.0945 to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <CNY=CFXS>> on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Under the managed float, the Chinese authorities allow the yuan to move within a 0.3 percent daily limit against the dollar and 1.5 percent against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 When the euro jumped sharply against the dollar last week, threatening a break by one or the other outside the daily limit against the yuan, rumour swirled in the market that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  was buying the dollar in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the range.  \par \par \uc2 Asked about that rumour, Watanabe said it depended on the definition of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "They do \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  every day if you want to put it that way. They have a centralised \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  system, in which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  absorbs all the unfilled buy orders or sell orders in the market at the end of the day," Watanabe said.  \par \par \uc2 "You might see the rates move around a lot 15-30 minutes before the close, and that's probably because the People's Bank of China is buying or selling to absorb the orders."  \par \par \uc2 He said he had already told his Beijing counterparts that setting different daily limits against different \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had inherent flaws.  \par \par \uc2 "It's not possible to have a 0.3 percent range against one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and 1.5 percent against another," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe said \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and interest rates, while a regular topic at G7 meetings, would be a smaller issue at the upcoming G7 meeting, compared with sky-high oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 "There are not a lot of concrete measures that finance ministers can decide on oil, but the situation has not improved from the last meeting and we need a broader approach," he said of discussions on oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 He declined to comment on how the wording on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the G7 communiqu\u233\'00\'E9 could be changed.  \par \par \uc2 "Merely changing some words can generate a lot of speculation, so we must be cautious," he said. The statements at recent G7 meetings called for more flexibility in exchange rates of countries with more rigid systems -- widely regarded as referring to China and some other Asian countries.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-JAPAN-WATANABE (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|J|NAT|U|Z|ABX|BNX|RNP|DNP|PTD  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050908e1980016e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
