\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  Yuan Ends Up Slightly; PBOC  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Suspected  \b0\par\par\uc2 395 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 2005\par 10:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yuan closed only slightly higher against the U.S. dollar Tuesday, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 In the spot market, the U.S. dollar ended at CNY8.0913, down from CNY8.0916 Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar traded between CNY8.0912 and CNY8.0919 during the session, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 "The rate has fallen a fair bit over the last few days. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may well have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow its progress," said a Shanghai-based dealer with a foreign bank.  \par \par \uc2 However, the dealer said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't aggressively propping up the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been emphasizing the role of market forces in setting the rate. Today's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably reflects this thinking (with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  less forcefully," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has been falling against the yuan since Wednesday last week, dropping 0.10% from Wednesday's close of CNY8.0998.  \par \par \uc2 After the July 21 revaluation, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officially allowed the pair a daily trading range of 0.3% from the previous day's closing level. However, the pair has yet to reach 0.3% above or below the original CNY8.1100 level to which the yuan was revalued. [ 06-09-05 0831GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 In the nondeliverable forwards market, traders said prices rose as the dollar strengthened against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and as market participants digested the news that China's President Hu Jintao had delayed his visit to Washington.  \par \par \uc2 The offshore bid/offer quote for the one-year dollar/yuan nondeliverable forwards was 7.8300/7.8450, up from 7.8250/7.8350 late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 A Hong Kong-based trader said NDF prices tracked the dollar-yen rate, rising as the dollar strengthened.  \par \par \uc2 Onshore, Industrial & Commercial Bank of China posted a one-year forwards price of 783.79 per 100 dollars on its Web site Tuesday morning, down from 784.34 Monday.  \par \par \uc2 In other spot trading pairs, the Hong Kong dollar ended down at CNY1.0413 from CNY1.0414; the Japanese yen fell to CNY7.3879 per 100 yen from CNY7.3938; and the euro dropped to CNY10.1040 from CNY10.1427.  \par \par \uc2 -By Denis McMahon, Dow Jones Newswires; (86-21) 6120-1200; denis.mcmahon@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Sharon Buan [ 06-09-05 0838GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1153  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050906e196000en\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
