\par\par \b \uc2 Bk Indonesia: $2 Billion China  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Swap Ops Pending  \b0\par\par\uc2 400 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 2005\par 13:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--Indonesia and China will likely reach a final agreement on a $2 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap agreement in the next two weeks, Bank Indonesia Governor Burhanuddin Abdullah said late Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "We expect that the Chinese government and the Indonesian government can have a final deal (on the $2 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap arrangement) within two weeks," Burhanuddin told reporters. [ 01-09-05 1125GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Burhanuddin said he hoped that the two sides would be able to sign a formal agreement at a meeting of finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs of the Group of 20 major industrialized countries in Beijing in October.  \par \par \uc2 The pending deal will double the size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap arrangement between China and Indonesia and will mark the completion of an agreement the two sides decided to accelerate in June.  \par \par \uc2 The agreement between the two countries' central banks allows them to borrow money from each other in times of crises. It is part of a number of such swap agreements set up under the so-called Chiang Mai Initiative, which aims to help member governments defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  during attacks of the sort the region experienced in 1997-98.  \par \par \uc2 Indonesia has an active interest in such arrangements due to a more than 11% slide in the value of the rupiah against the dollar since January that has defied more than $5 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  since April.  \par \par \uc2 In May, finance ministers from Japan, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , South Korea and the Association of Southeast Asian Nations agreed to increase the size of their US$39.5 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -swap program, and individual nations have since been negotiating terms of their particular deals. South Korea, for instance, has expanded its swap agreements with Japan and China as the previous arrangements expired.  \par \par \uc2 Indonesia's pending \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap deal with China follows Burhanuddin's announcement in Tokyo Wednesday of a second bilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  agreement to allow \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps of up to $6 billion between monetary authorities in Indonesia and Japan.  \par \par \uc2 That pact also increased to 20% from 10% the percentage of a swap line available without an International Monetary Fund economic reform agreement or being close to reaching such an IMF agreement.  \par \par \uc2 Indonesia ended its IMF program at the end of 2004.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jakarta Bureau; Dow Jones Newswires; 62 21 3983 1277; dj.jakarta@dowjones.com [ 01-09-05 1151GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050901e191000mv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
