\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-INTERVIEW-China cbank adviser says sharp yuan rise risky  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kevin Yao and Victoria Bi  \par \uc2 608 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 August 2005\par 03:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Repeating story from Friday)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Aug 26 (Reuters) - China is trying to make the yuan more flexible to reflect market supply and demand, but any sharp rise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be dangerous to the fast-growing economy, an influential Chinese economist said on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Yu Yongding, head of the Institute of the World Economics and Politics at the Chinese Academy of Social Sciences, said last month's mild yuan revaluation could hit some exports, but would help reduce the economy's over-reliance on external demand.  \par \par \uc2 "In future, we will allow market forces to play a bigger role in setting the exchange rate, but we can never rule out market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Yu told Reuters in an interview.  \par \par \uc2 Yu is the only academic member of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy committee, which advises on, but does not set, policy.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't advocate a big, one-off revaluation as I believe such a move will be very dangerous," Yu said.  \par \par \uc2 Speculation swirled in financial markets on Friday that a further revaluation was imminent but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  denied that such a move would come soon.  \par \par \uc2 China revalued the yuan <CNY=CFXS> by 2.1 percent against the dollar on July 21, scrapped a decade-long peg against the dollar and put the yuan under a managed float with reference to a basket of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the dollar, euro and yen.  \par \par \uc2 Yu, who described the basket system as transitional, said the new regime made it harder for speculators to bet on the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 "The basket system is transitional and I hope government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be reduced gradually," he said. "Other things being equal, the basket system will help restrain speculation as they can no longer put a one-way bet."  \par \par \uc2 Under the managed float, the yuan can rise or fall against the dollar by as much as 0.3 percent a day.  \par \par \uc2 MILD SLOWDOWN  \par \par \uc2 So far the yuan, whose value remains closely guarded by the the People's Bank of China, has not come close to using even that modest leeway -- due mainly, analysts say, to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Yu said \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves were expected to continue to rise sharply this year, partly fuelled by a hefty current account surplus that was likely to hit at least $80 billion this year, despite the yuan revaluation.  \par \par \uc2 China's reserves, the world's largest after Japan's, rose $51.9 billion in the second quarter to a record $711 billion.  \par \par \uc2 China's trade surplus in the first seven months of 2005 was $50 billion, already dwarfing the surplus for all of 2004 of $32 billion. China's current account surplus last year jumped 50 percent to $68.7 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Yu said the economy was likely to slow due to weakening domestic demand, reflected in weaker inflation and profit growth in recent months, but it was too early for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to relax monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 "We should be vigilant as economic growth could lose its momentum. But I believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should keep monetary policy stable. There is no need to relax," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Economic growth, which was a robust 9.5 percent in the first half, was on track to reach about 9.0 percent for the whole year and any sharp slowdown next year was unlikely, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Yu said the government should use its fiscal policy to stimulate domestic demand to help cushion any economic slowdown.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-CHINA-YUAN-YING|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|O|OIL|Z  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050829e18t0001v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
