\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  Yuan Ends Down;  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervention \highlight\uc2  Suspected  \b0\par\par\uc2 492 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 August 2005\par 11:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2005, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yuan closed lower against the U.S. dollar Monday, after suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the yuan from appreciating significantly.  \par \par \uc2 In the spot market, the U.S. dollar ended up at CNY8.1056, the highest close since Aug. 10, after trading between CNY8.1057 and CNY8.1044. It closed at CNY8.1047 Friday.  \par \par \uc2 A large dollar bid at the CNY8.1045 level made it difficult for the dollar/yuan pair to fall further, said a Shenzhen trader. He said the bid was too large to be made by any other participant than the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The dollar/yuan pair has risen strongly since closing at a post-revaluation low of CNY8.0971 on Aug. 15.  \par \par \uc2 Since the yuan's 2.1% revaluation against the dollar last month, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been suspected on several occasions of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The yuan has never come close to the 0.3% daily trading limit permitted against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The offshore bid/offer quote for the one-year dollar/yuan nondeliverable forwards was 7.8350/7.8450, down from 7.8600/7.8650 late Friday.  \par \par \uc2 A Singapore trader said that investors 'played the downside' after recent NDF highs but said that the 1-year NDFs would likely stay close to their current level for the near future.  \par \par \uc2 Onshore, China Construction Bank posted a one-year forward dollar bid of 7.8550 for the dollar/yuan, based on the official Chinamoney.com.cn. Web site, which is operated by the China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trading System.  \par \par \uc2 The onshore dollar/yuan forward compared with a dollar bid quote of 7.8550 Friday. No trades were done Monday, according to the Web site.  \par \par \uc2 China's Securities Journal reported Monday that China has had only three \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  forward transactions through the interbank market since the People's Bank of China issued rules allowing such trading.  \par \par \uc2 It said potential participants are still awaiting more detailed rules.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Bank of China, one of China's four largest state commercial banks, said Monday it plans to launch South Korean won-Chinese yuan cash exchange services next month, to meet demand from individuals for the exchange of the two \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That follows a similar offering from Industrial & Commercial Bank of China.  \par \par \uc2 South Korea has become one of China's biggest trading partners, and the won is among the key \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  making up the basket to which China references the value of the yuan. But the news didn't have an impact on the market Monday.  \par \par \uc2 In other spot trading pairs, the Hong Kong dollar ended up at CNY1.0426 from CNY1.0423; the Japanese yen rose to CNY7.3617 per 100 yen from CNY7.3257; and the euro fell to CNY9.8703 from CNY9.8706. -By Daniel Pescatore, Dow Jones Newswires; (86-21) 6120-1200; daniel.pescatore@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by David Riordan  \par \par \uc2 - -  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020050822e18m006ps\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
