\par\par \b \uc2 OUTLOOK: Huangpu HSFO Imports To Rise But Demand May Lag  \b0\par\par\uc2 814 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2005\par 11:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Energy Service\par \uc2 NRG\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--South China's fuel oil imports are expected to gradually pick up this month as import costs fall, due to a stronger yuan and a recent tariff cut in fuel oil imports from Singapore.  \par \par \uc2 But lower import costs haven't meant lower prices for end users, who continue to be vexed by high fuel oil prices, especially for the mixed/cracked grade.  \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the U.S. dollar closed at CNY8.1051 in China's onshore \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, after hitting a new low of CNY8,1025 since the yuan's revaluation July 21.  \par \par \uc2 The uptrend in the yuan - which has boosted the buying power of Chinese importers - is likely to persist in the short term, barring any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 More good news for fuel oil importers. China recently cut tariffs on fuel oil cargoes from Singapore to 5% from 6%, which would save import costs by some CNY20/ton based on current prices.  \par \par \uc2 Chinese importers are also buying now to take advantage of relatively low fuel oil prices in Singapore, which have eased mainly due to ample supply.  \par \par \uc2 Import costs for Singapore fuel oil have been largely below CNY2,800 a metric ton, while in the Huangpu market, the latest prompt price is around CNY2,900-CNY2,920/ton, promising importers a handsome profit of more than CNY100/ton.  \par \par \uc2 About 805,000 tons of fuel oil cargoes are slated to arrive in Huangpu this month, according to the latest tanker fixtures.  \par \par \uc2 With the cargo-fixing window still open, traders expect overall imports in August to surpass 1 million tons, compared with 968,000 tons in July.  \par \par \uc2 Particularly, Singapore fuel oil cargoes in the Huangpu market will see a jump from July. The latest tanker fixtures put them at 380,000 tons in August, compared with 287,000 tons last month.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Demand For Mixed/Cracked Fuel Oil Still Slack \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While overall fuel oil supply in the near term looks set to rise, demand may not necessarily be as enthusiastic, governed by different concerns.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Buying interest for mixed/cracked fuel oil, which is used by power plants, ceramics factories and chemical plants, remains muted, as prices are still well above what end users can afford despite the yuan's revaluation and the tariff cut.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In theory, an appreciation in the yuan and a tariff cut should lead to a drop in prompt prices. But prices have been rising slowly instead, pulled up by surging Western crude futures.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With crude futures expected to stay high in the short term, fuel oil prices in the spot market will also follow suit, further damping end user buying, a local trader said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At current prices, profits for Chinese end users are next to impossible.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indeed, fuel oil-fired power plants in Guangdong province are trying to lower their operating rates as much as possible because they are unable to make any profits, despite severe power shortages in the southern province.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders estimate the break-even point for power plants is around CNY2,700-CNY2,750/ton, inclusive of government subsidies.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Wednesday, the prompt price for cracked 180 cst fuel oil was quoted at CNY2,900-CNY2,920/ton on a ship-to-ship basis, unchanged from Tuesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the outlook for straight-run fuel oil is brighter as refining margins widen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Guangdong, many small independent refineries, also known as teapot refineries, have been running at high capacity since the Chinese government raised the retail price of diesel by CNY250/ton July 23.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Small refineries usually use straight-run fuel oil as feedstock for secondary cracking into diesel.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some refineries, though still shut for now, have begun to rebuild their inventories to resume \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as soon as possible, said a second trader.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders expect diesel consumption will be in full swing September-to-October with the end of a seasonal fishing ban, boosting demand for straight-run fuel oil.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An annual ban on fishing in South China Sea was lifted Aug. 1, and in all coastal areas, the ban will come to an end Sept. 16. Many fishing boats run on off-specification diesel.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But demand for straight-run fuel oil may slow if price gains in off-spec diesel are smaller than expected, some traders said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meantime, due to low importer inventories, traders said Huangpu will see higher imports for straight-run fuel oil in the weeks ahead. But so far, it has been hard to forecast an exact number.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the HSFO C&F Huangpu price assessment, go to or search keyword Huangpu or code N/PET. For the Huangpu fuel oil market commentary, go to or search keyword Huangpu or code N/RMKT.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Sherry Su, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-64154065; sherry.su@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Ryan Woo [ 08-03-05 0505ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 28099  \par \par \uc2 Dokument NRG0000020050803e1830000f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
