\par\par \b \uc2 Swelling reserves may prod China into more  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  steps  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 716 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 2005\par 19:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Aug 2 (Reuters) - The rapid growth in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves could force a further rise in the yuan soon despite Beijing's insistence that its July 21 revaluation does not mean further moves are likely.  \par \par \uc2 The longer China waits to further liberalize the yuan, seen as a key step toward fixing global economic imbalances, the bigger the capital loss on its international reserves will be, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 "This is very difficult to judge. If they wait longer, their reserves will be larger," said Nicholas Lardy, senior fellow at the Institute for International Economics in Washington.  \par \par \uc2 The one-time 2.1 percent adjustment, which resulted from China scrapping the yuan's peg of around 8.28 to the dollar in favor of tying it to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , wiped an estimated $11 billion from China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.  \par \par \uc2 That assumes China holds around 75 percent of its $711 billion international reserves in dollar-denominated assets.  \par \par \uc2 Lardy expects the yuan to appreciate a further 1 percent to 2 percent in coming weeks, but would be surprised if it revalues more than 5 percent to 6 percent by the end of the year.  \par \par \uc2 A 5 percent to 6 percent revaluation, all other things being equal, would wipe another $32 billion off the value of China's reserves at current yuan levels. That is around 1.8 percent of gross domestic product.  \par \par \uc2 China's reserves are the second-largest in the world after Japan's $843.5 billion. But as a share of GDP, China's reserves are around 39 percent, compared with Japan's 18 percent.  \par \par \uc2 They have surged from $403 billion at the end of 2003 and $610 billion at the end of 2004, a rate of growth much faster than the rate of China's overall economic growth of around 9 percent to 10 percent a year.  \par \par \uc2 With a widening trade surplus, stellar capital inflows and persistent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to steady the yuan, there is no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves diminishing.  \par \par \uc2 EURO HOLDINGS RISING TOO  \par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves could be used by the government to spur domestic demand and help wean the economy off export-reliant growth.  \par \par \uc2 "But you're limited because if you want to keep the 8.11 rate, you have to carry on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Nouriel Roubini, professor of economics at New York University, referring to the yuan's new reference rate per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "The more you wait, the more you have reserves accumulating and the more losses (on those reserves) you have," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Peoples Bank of China, is now targeting a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  instead of just the U.S. dollar. But the composition and weighting of the basket is unknown.  \par \par \uc2 All the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said is that it contains at least four \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 : the U.S. dollar, euro, yen and Hong Kong dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Similarly, the composition of China's reserves is unknown. Estimates vary, but it is thought that anywhere between 20 percent and 40 percent may be held in non-dollar-denominated assets.  \par \par \uc2 Global central banks are thought to be undertaking a slow, gradual shift to increase their non-U.S. dollar holdings.  \par \par \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Monday it has increased the euro share of its target \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket to 0.35 from 0.30.  \par \par \uc2 This trend, which China has almost certainly been actively involved in, has been an important factor behind the dollar's broad decline since January 2002, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 Stephen Jen, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Morgan Stanley in London, said on Monday that the "usual suspects" may be "redistributing" their reserves with the euro at its current $1.20-$1.22 rate rather than $1.30-$1.32 earlier this year.  \par \par \uc2 "But there's no technical reason to have your reserves composition correspond to the composition of different \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a basket," said Lardy at the IIE.  \par \par \uc2 Roubini agrees, saying that despite talk that China has been buying euros lately, the dollar will still be the principal conduit for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , thereby ensuring that the dollar component of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves remains high.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -YUAN-RESERVES|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|J|Z|ABX|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050802e182001v5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
